{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Ks7NAC72iuD35DQDJQyM8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log\n2\t\n3\tTicket: abcd-04 | Phase: architect (`arch-research-stack`) | Status: complete\n4\t\n5\tBrownfield PRD-04 (Notifications & Email) on top of PRD-00..03. Per\n6\t`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` (confidence 100%) the base stack is fixed:\n7\tNext.js 14 (App Router), React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9 (+emotion) and\n8\tMUI X, Auth.js v5, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Zod, React Hook Form, Vitest/Testing\n9\tLibrary/Playwright. `handoff-to-architect.json` reports 97% confidence and no\n10\tblockers. This PRD's only real stack question is the email layer (FR-7..13);\n11\teverything else slots into the existing app. NFR-10 explicitly caps scope\n12\t(no SMS/push/websockets/digests), which rules out several heavier options\n13\tbelow.\n14\t\n15\t## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack, no new framework\n16\t\n17\t- **Context:** FR-1..FR-24 are additive feature work inside an existing\n18\t  brownfield app; NFR-10 forbids scope expansion (no SMS/push, no realtime\n19\t  websockets, no digest emails).\n20\t- **Decision:** Build entirely on the detected stack (Next.js API routes,\n21\t  Prisma, MUI, Zod, RHF, Auth.js). No new runtime, ORM, or UI framework.\n22\t- **Consequences:** Zero migration cost; new code must match existing `src/`\n23\t  conventions (response envelopes, `requireUser`/`requireRole` from PRD-00/01).\n24\t\n25\t## ADR-2: Nodemailer as the one SMTP client, promoted to a direct dependency\n26\t\n27\t- **Context:** FR-10/FR-13 need an SMTP sender with env-driven config\n28\t  (`SMTP_HOST`/`PORT`/`USER`/`PASS`/`SECURE`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp|console`).\n29\t  `nodemailer` is already present in `package-lock.json` only as a transitive\n30\t  dependency of `next-auth`'s email provider plumbing — it is not a direct\n31\t  dependency and must not be assumed available at a pinned version.\n32\t- **Decision:** Add `nodemailer` (+ `@types/nodemailer`) as a direct\n33\t  dependency. One transport module (`src/server/email/transport.ts`) builds\n34\t  either a real SMTP transport or a `console` transport (logs subject,\n35\t  recipient, text body) based on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`, defaulting to `console`\n36\t  in development per FR-13.\n37\t- **Consequences:** No ambiguity about which nodemailer version ships; no\n38\t  second mail library introduced for the console/dev path.\n39\t\n40\t## ADR-3: @react-email/components + @react-email/render for templates\n41\t\n42\t- **Context:** FR-7/FR-8 require branded templates (shared base layout,\n43\t  invite/password-reset/generic) rendered to **both** HTML and plain text,\n44\t  built with `@react-email/components` per NFR-1 (email templates are the\n45\t  one UI surface explicitly exempted from the MUI-only rule, since MUI isn't\n46\t  email-client-safe markup).\n47\t- **Decision:** Add `@react-email/components` and `@react-email/render` as\n48\t  direct dependencies. Each template is a React component under\n49\t  `src/server/email/templates/`, sharing one `BaseLayout` component (logo,\n50\t  content slot, footer with address + disclosure + preferences link). Render\n51\t  call sites use `render(<Template .../>)` for HTML and\n52\t  `render(<Template .../>, { plainText: true })` for the text part — no\n53\t  separate hand-written text templates, so the two parts can't drift.\n54\t- **Consequences:** One rendering path backs both EmailOutbox columns\n55\t  (`html`, `text`); template changes automatically stay HTML/text consistent.\n56\t\n57\t## ADR-4: EmailOutbox dispatch via raw `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, no queue library\n58\t\n59\t- **Context:** FR-10/NFR-4 require concurrency-safe batch claiming (no email\n60\t  sent twice by two dispatchers) without a message broker — Prisma's query\n61\t  builder has no `SKIP LOCKED` support, and NFR-10 rules out adding\n62\t  infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ/SQS) for a feature capped at polling-scale\n63\t  volume.\n64\t- **Decision:** `dispatchEmails()` uses one `prisma.$transaction` containing\n65\t  a `$queryRaw` `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` to claim a batch of\n66\t  PENDING rows (flip to SENDING), then sends each via Nodemailer outside the\n67\t  lock, then updates status (SENT, or retry backoff per FR-11, or FAILED\n68\t  after attempt 5). A second periodic query recovers rows stuck in SENDING\n69\t  for >10 minutes back to PENDING (NFR-4).\n70\t- **Consequences:** No new infra; the only raw SQL in the codebase is this\n71\t  one claim query, isolated behind `dispatchEmails()` — every other read/\n72\t  write stays on the Prisma client API.\n73\t\n74\t## ADR-5: Two thin drivers over one dispatch function — HTTP route + loop script, no scheduler\n75\t\n76\t- **Context:** FR-12 needs both an externally-cron-triggered HTTP endpoint\n77\t  (`POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`, bearer-guarded by `CRON_SECRET`,\n78\t  NFR-8) and a `npm run worker:email` script that loops every 15s for local/\n79\t  simple deployments.\n80\t- **Decision:** Both are thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()` (ADR-4).\n81\t  The loop script is a plain `setInterval`-driven Node script\n82\t  (`scripts/worker-email.ts` run via `tsx`, matching the existing\n83\t  `db:seed` pattern) — no cron library, no job-scheduler dependency.\n84\t- **Consequences:** One dispatch implementation, two callers; adding a real\n85\t  scheduler later (if deployment target changes) only touches the driver,\n86\t  not `dispatchEmails()`.\n87\t\n88\t## ADR-6: Bell + preferences UI stays polling, built from existing MUI/MUI X — no new UI deps\n89\t\n90\t- **Context:** FR-14 (unread-count bell poll every 30s, paused on\n91\t  `visibilitychange`), FR-15 (Popover + List), FR-16 (/notifications list +\n92\t  filters), FR-18 (preferences table with per-row Switches) are all additive\n93\t  screens; NFR-1 mandates MUI-only, and NFR-10 explicitly rules out\n94\t  websockets.\n95\t- **Decision:** Plain `fetch` + `setInterval`/`document.visibilityState` for\n96\t  polling (no SWR/React Query/socket library added). All visible elements\n97\t  come from already-installed `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`\n98\t  (Badge, IconButton, Popover, List, Table, Switch, Tabs) — same ESLint\n99\t  `no-restricted-imports` MUI-only gate as prior PRDs enforces this.\n100\t- **Consequences:** Zero new frontend dependencies; one shared polling hook\n101\t  (e.g. `useUnreadCount`) can back both the bell and, if ever needed, the\n102\t  list page — no premature abstraction beyond that single reuse point.\n103\t\n104\t## ADR-7: Notification type registry is a plain TS const + literal union, not a DB table\n105\t\n106\t- **Context:** FR-2/FR-3 need a typed registry (key → label/description/\n107\t  category/defaults/roles) where `notify()` rejects unknown type keys **at\n108\t  compile time**.\n109\t- **Decision:** `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` is a `const` object in\n110\t  `src/server/notifications/types.ts`; its key union\n111\t  (`keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) is the type parameter `notify()`\n112\t  accepts. No database-backed type table, no runtime registry lookup for\n113\t  validity — compile-time exhaustiveness is free and stronger than a runtime\n114\t  check.\n115\t- **Consequences:** Adding a notification type is a code change + PR review\n116\t  (desired: it drives UI copy and default routing), not a migration or admin\n117\t  screen.\n118\t\n119\t## ADR-8: purgeOldNotifications scheduling stays in-memory, no job table\n120\t\n121\t- **Context:** FR-17 needs a daily purge of notifications >180 days old;\n122\t  explicitly \"acceptable to re-run after a restart.\"\n123\t- **Decision:** Track last-run time in a module-level variable inside the\n124\t  dispatch route handler (checked on each `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`\n125\t  call, which already fires every 15s–few minutes via the cron/worker); no\n126\t  `CronJob`/`LastRun` table, no separate scheduler.\n127\t- **Consequences:** Matches the stated tolerance for restart-driven re-runs;\n128\t  avoids a persistence concept this PRD doesn't otherwise need.\n129\t\n130\t## ADR-9: MUI-only UI surface, enforced by the existing ESLint rule (carried over)\n131\t\n132\t- **Context:** NFR-1 requires every visible element to come from\n133\t  `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`/MUI X, with banned libraries blocked\n134\t  by `no-restricted-imports`; email templates are the sole, explicit\n135\t  exception (ADR-3).\n136\t- **Decision:** Reuse the PRD-00 ESLint config as-is for all app UI; the\n137\t  exception glob only needs to cover `src/server/email/templates/**` so\n138\t  `@react-email/components` imports aren't flagged there.\n139\t- **Consequences:** Consistent look/feel with PRD-00..03; the one new\n140\t  dependency surface (react-email) is scoped and fenced by lint, not by\n141\t  convention alone.\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecdd0a7a-9233-4799-bc04-313a457b4439","uuid":"2875191f-147e-41ba-9f1d-40d86da07f9a","timestamp":"2026-10-19T10:08:21.407Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — ADR Log\n\nTicket: abcd-04 | Phase: architect (`arch-research-stack`) | Status: complete\n\nBrownfield PRD-04 (Notifications & Email) on top of PRD-00..03. Per\n`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` (confidence 100%) the base stack is fixed:\nNext.js 14 (App Router), React 18, TypeScript strict, MUI v9 (+emotion) and\nMUI X, Auth.js v5, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Zod, React Hook Form, Vitest/Testing\nLibrary/Playwright. `handoff-to-architect.json` reports 97% confidence and no\nblockers. This PRD's only real stack question is the email layer (FR-7..13);\neverything else slots into the existing app. NFR-10 explicitly caps scope\n(no SMS/push/websockets/digests), which rules out several heavier options\nbelow.\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack, no new framework\n\n- **Context:** FR-1..FR-24 are additive feature work inside an existing\n  brownfield app; NFR-10 forbids scope expansion (no SMS/push, no realtime\n  websockets, no digest emails).\n- **Decision:** Build entirely on the detected stack (Next.js API routes,\n  Prisma, MUI, Zod, RHF, Auth.js). No new runtime, ORM, or UI framework.\n- **Consequences:** Zero migration cost; new code must match existing `src/`\n  conventions (response envelopes, `requireUser`/`requireRole` from PRD-00/01).\n\n## ADR-2: Nodemailer as the one SMTP client, promoted to a direct dependency\n\n- **Context:** FR-10/FR-13 need an SMTP sender with env-driven config\n  (`SMTP_HOST`/`PORT`/`USER`/`PASS`/`SECURE`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp|console`).\n  `nodemailer` is already present in `package-lock.json` only as a transitive\n  dependency of `next-auth`'s email provider plumbing — it is not a direct\n  dependency and must not be assumed available at a pinned version.\n- **Decision:** Add `nodemailer` (+ `@types/nodemailer`) as a direct\n  dependency. One transport module (`src/server/email/transport.ts`) builds\n  either a real SMTP transport or a `console` transport (logs subject,\n  recipient, text body) based on `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`, defaulting to `console`\n  in development per FR-13.\n- **Consequences:** No ambiguity about which nodemailer version ships; no\n  second mail library introduced for the console/dev path.\n\n## ADR-3: @react-email/components + @react-email/render for templates\n\n- **Context:** FR-7/FR-8 require branded templates (shared base layout,\n  invite/password-reset/generic) rendered to **both** HTML and plain text,\n  built with `@react-email/components` per NFR-1 (email templates are the\n  one UI surface explicitly exempted from the MUI-only rule, since MUI isn't\n  email-client-safe markup).\n- **Decision:** Add `@react-email/components` and `@react-email/render` as\n  direct dependencies. Each template is a React component under\n  `src/server/email/templates/`, sharing one `BaseLayout` component (logo,\n  content slot, footer with address + disclosure + preferences link). Render\n  call sites use `render(<Template .../>)` for HTML and\n  `render(<Template .../>, { plainText: true })` for the text part — no\n  separate hand-written text templates, so the two parts can't drift.\n- **Consequences:** One rendering path backs both EmailOutbox columns\n  (`html`, `text`); template changes automatically stay HTML/text consistent.\n\n## ADR-4: EmailOutbox dispatch via raw `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, no queue library\n\n- **Context:** FR-10/NFR-4 require concurrency-safe batch claiming (no email\n  sent twice by two dispatchers) without a message broker — Prisma's query\n  builder has no `SKIP LOCKED` support, and NFR-10 rules out adding\n  infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ/SQS) for a feature capped at polling-scale\n  volume.\n- **Decision:** `dispatchEmails()` uses one `prisma.$transaction` containing\n  a `$queryRaw` `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` to claim a batch of\n  PENDING rows (flip to SENDING), then sends each via Nodemailer outside the\n  lock, then updates status (SENT, or retry backoff per FR-11, or FAILED\n  after attempt 5). A second periodic query recovers rows stuck in SENDING\n  for >10 minutes back to PENDING (NFR-4).\n- **Consequences:** No new infra; the only raw SQL in the codebase is this\n  one claim query, isolated behind `dispatchEmails()` — every other read/\n  write stays on the Prisma client API.\n\n## ADR-5: Two thin drivers over one dispatch function — HTTP route + loop script, no scheduler\n\n- **Context:** FR-12 needs both an externally-cron-triggered HTTP endpoint\n  (`POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`, bearer-guarded by `CRON_SECRET`,\n  NFR-8) and a `npm run worker:email` script that loops every 15s for local/\n  simple deployments.\n- **Decision:** Both are thin callers of the same `dispatchEmails()` (ADR-4).\n  The loop script is a plain `setInterval`-driven Node script\n  (`scripts/worker-email.ts` run via `tsx`, matching the existing\n  `db:seed` pattern) — no cron library, no job-scheduler dependency.\n- **Consequences:** One dispatch implementation, two callers; adding a real\n  scheduler later (if deployment target changes) only touches the driver,\n  not `dispatchEmails()`.\n\n## ADR-6: Bell + preferences UI stays polling, built from existing MUI/MUI X — no new UI deps\n\n- **Context:** FR-14 (unread-count bell poll every 30s, paused on\n  `visibilitychange`), FR-15 (Popover + List), FR-16 (/notifications list +\n  filters), FR-18 (preferences table with per-row Switches) are all additive\n  screens; NFR-1 mandates MUI-only, and NFR-10 explicitly rules out\n  websockets.\n- **Decision:** Plain `fetch` + `setInterval`/`document.visibilityState` for\n  polling (no SWR/React Query/socket library added). All visible elements\n  come from already-installed `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`\n  (Badge, IconButton, Popover, List, Table, Switch, Tabs) — same ESLint\n  `no-restricted-imports` MUI-only gate as prior PRDs enforces this.\n- **Consequences:** Zero new frontend dependencies; one shared polling hook\n  (e.g. `useUnreadCount`) can back both the bell and, if ever needed, the\n  list page — no premature abstraction beyond that single reuse point.\n\n## ADR-7: Notification type registry is a plain TS const + literal union, not a DB table\n\n- **Context:** FR-2/FR-3 need a typed registry (key → label/description/\n  category/defaults/roles) where `notify()` rejects unknown type keys **at\n  compile time**.\n- **Decision:** `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` is a `const` object in\n  `src/server/notifications/types.ts`; its key union\n  (`keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`) is the type parameter `notify()`\n  accepts. No database-backed type table, no runtime registry lookup for\n  validity — compile-time exhaustiveness is free and stronger than a runtime\n  check.\n- **Consequences:** Adding a notification type is a code change + PR review\n  (desired: it drives UI copy and default routing), not a migration or admin\n  screen.\n\n## ADR-8: purgeOldNotifications scheduling stays in-memory, no job table\n\n- **Context:** FR-17 needs a daily purge of notifications >180 days old;\n  explicitly \"acceptable to re-run after a restart.\"\n- **Decision:** Track last-run time in a module-level variable inside the\n  dispatch route handler (checked on each `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch`\n  call, which already fires every 15s–few minutes via the cron/worker); no\n  `CronJob`/`LastRun` table, no separate scheduler.\n- **Consequences:** Matches the stated tolerance for restart-driven re-runs;\n  avoids a persistence concept this PRD doesn't otherwise need.\n\n## ADR-9: MUI-only UI surface, enforced by the existing ESLint rule (carried over)\n\n- **Context:** NFR-1 requires every visible element to come from\n  `@mui/material`/`@mui/icons-material`/MUI X, with banned libraries blocked\n  by `no-restricted-imports`; email templates are the sole, explicit\n  exception (ADR-3).\n- **Decision:** Reuse the PRD-00 ESLint config as-is for all app UI; the\n  exception glob only needs to cover `src/server/email/templates/**` so\n  `@react-email/components` imports aren't flagged there.\n- **Consequences:** Consistent look/feel with PRD-00..03; the one new\n  dependency surface (react-email) is scoped and fenced by lint, not by\n  convention alone.\n","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
