s (academic years, subjects, rooms, grade levels, users). Architect should decide: add a lightweight `group` field to NavItem, or ship Sections/Courses as flat entries for now.\",\n    \"NFR-7 (MEDIUM confidence): src/server/auth/permissions.ts and requireUser() described in Platform Context do not exist yet — only requireRole.ts and student-access.ts are present. Same brownfield gap PRD-04's architect handoff flagged; new routes should keep using the requireRole(...ALL_ROLES) stand-in until PRD-01 lands.\"\n  ],\n  \"nextPhase\": \"architect\"\n}\n","numLines":16,"startLine":1,"totalLines":16}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CgT4RRX6zYkrEutW9bdJG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XqpHynHG4oCVMRC8dxU8BY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1289,"cache_read_input_tokens":27096,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1289},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6adde39-f92b-490f-9ef1-b8393dd3c9ab","uuid":"5da9efb3-e7c5-4e13-9156-2a172fc53b57","timestamp":"2026-10-27T16:30:17.865Z","request_id":"req_011CgT4RRHCtLtkcip7wnDpW"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqpHynHG4oCVMRC8dxU8BY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-05 Classes & Enrollment\n2\t\n3\tTicket: abcd-05 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n4\t\n5\tBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\n6\tNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\n7\tMUI v9 + emotion, `zod` for input validation, `date-fns`. This PRD is\n8\tbrownfield on top of that stack — intake's detection notes already confirm\n9\tit \"adds no new frontend framework or dependency,\" only new Prisma models\n10\tand pages. The decisions below are the implementation-pattern deltas this\n11\tPRD's requirements force, not a re-litigation of the base stack. No new\n12\truntime dependency is added by this PRD.\n13\t\n14\t## ADR-1: Bulk enroll/drop writes — `Prisma.$transaction` with batched\n15\t`createMany`/`updateMany`, no job queue\n16\t\n17\t**Context.** NFR-3 requires adding 30 students to a section with 8 core\n18\tcourses (240 resulting enrollments) to complete in under 1 second. FR-7–\n19\tFR-10 describe enroll-on-add, drop-on-remove, move-section, and\n20\tcreate-core-course as all-or-nothing operations touching many `Enrollment`\n21\trows at once.\n22\t\n23\t**Decision.** Implement each of these as a single `prisma.$transaction`\n24\tthat computes the affected (studentId, courseId) pairs in memory first,\n25\tthen issues one `enrollment.createMany({ skipDuplicates: true })` for new\n26\tACTIVE rows and one `enrollment.updateMany` (keyed on the unique\n27\t`[courseId, studentId]`) to reactivate previously-DROPPED rows, instead of\n28\tlooping `create`/`update` calls per student per course. This is plain\n29\tPrisma batch-write API already in the installed `@prisma/client` — no\n30\tqueue, no background worker, no new dependency. 240 rows in one\n31\t`createMany` plus one `updateMany` comfortably clears the 1-second bar on\n32\tPostgres; a per-row loop (240 round-trips) would not.\n33\t\n34\t**Consequences.** Business logic that decides \"create vs. reactivate\" for\n35\teach pair must be computed with a single prior `findMany`, not per-row\n36\tqueries, to keep the whole operation at a small constant number of\n37\tround-trips. If a future PRD needs enroll/drop across thousands of rows\n38\t(bulk import), that's a new ADR for chunking or a background job — not a\n39\tretrofit of this one.\n40\t\n41\t_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-3_\n42\t\n43\t## ADR-2: Capacity + uniqueness enforcement — DB unique constraints for\n44\tidentity, in-transaction count check for capacity, no distributed lock\n45\t\n46\t**Context.** NFR-2 requires the one-section-per-student-per-year rule to be\n47\ta database constraint, not just app logic. FR-11 requires capacity\n48\tenforcement (section and course) that refuses with CONFLICT unless an\n49\texplicit \"Override capacity\" flag is set, under concurrent admin actions.\n50\t\n51\t**Decision.** Identity/duplication rules (`@@unique([studentId,\n52\tacademicYearId])` on `SectionMember`, `@@unique([courseId, studentId])` on\n53\t`Enrollment`) are enforced by Postgres unique indexes already specified in\n54\tFR-1; application code catches the Prisma `P2002` violation and maps it to\n55\tthe existing CONFLICT error envelope rather than pre-checking existence\n56\tfirst (avoids a check-then-insert race). Capacity, which is a *computed*\n57\tcount rather than a stored invariant, is checked inside the same\n58\t`$transaction` that performs the insert — `count()` the current\n59\tmembers/enrollments, compare to `capacity`, and only then\n60\t`create`/`createMany`, so a concurrent request either serializes behind the\n61\ttransaction or re-checks and fails cleanly. No advisory lock, no Redis, no\n62\tdistributed-lock library: Postgres's own transactional read-your-writes\n63\twithin one `$transaction` is sufficient at this app's scale (a school\n64\tadmin console, not a high-concurrency storefront).\n65\t\n66\t**Consequences.** Capacity checks are \"good enough\" serialization, not a\n67\thard guarantee under extreme concurrent load from multiple admins hitting\n68\tthe exact same section simultaneously; acceptable because this is an\n69\tinternal admin tool with low write concurrency. If that ever changes,\n70\tupgrading the capacity check to `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on a row is a small\n71\tfollow-up, not a new ADR.\n72\t\n73\t_Cites: FR-1, FR-11, NFR-2_\n74\t\n75\t## ADR-3: No new dependency for the admin UI (tables, dialogs, pickers)\n76\t\n77\t**Context.** NFR-1 mandates every visible element in the sections/courses/\n78\tenrollment screens come from `@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`, or MUI\n79\tX, with no banned libraries (Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Radix, react-select,\n80\tetc.). FR-2–FR-6, FR-13–FR-19 describe filtered tables, create/edit\n81\tdialogs, multi-select pickers, tabs, and card grids.\n82\t\n83\t**Decision.** Build all of it from the already-installed MUI v9 set plus\n84\tthe existing shared components (`DataTable`, `UserPicker`, `ConfirmDialog`,\n85\t`useToast`, `PageHeader`) per the brownfield reuse already confirmed in\n86\tintake. Multi-select student/subject pickers extend `UserPicker`'s\n87\texisting `role` filter pattern rather than introducing a new\n88\tautocomplete/select component. No new UI dependency.\n89\t\n90\t**Consequences.** None beyond the existing MUI-MCP lookup discipline\n91\t(NFR-1) for any component variant not already used elsewhere in the\n92\tcodebase.\n93\t\n94\t_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-6, FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-17, FR-19, NFR-1_\n95\t\n96\t## ADR-4: Auth — keep the `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in, no new\n97\tauth helper\n98\t\n99\t**Context.** NFR-7 flags that `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` and\n100\t`requireUser()` described in the platform context don't exist yet — only\n101\t`requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present. This is the same\n102\tbrownfield gap PRD-04's architect handoff already flagged.\n103\t\n104\t**Decision.** New route handlers in this PRD (sections/courses/enrollment\n105\tCRUD, move-section, roster) continue to use the existing\n106\t`requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)`-style stand-in, and `assertCanViewCourse`/\n107\t`assertCanManageCourse` (FR-18) are added as plain functions in\n108\t`src/server/auth/course-access.ts` alongside the existing\n109\t`student-access.ts`, not as part of a new `permissions.ts` module. No new\n110\tauth library, no new abstraction invented ahead of PRD-01 landing it\n111\tproperly.\n112\t\n113\t**Consequences.** Role-gating logic stays slightly duplicated across\n114\tper-feature `*-access.ts` files until PRD-01 ships a real\n115\t`permissions.ts`; that consolidation is PRD-01's job, not this one's.\n116\t\n117\t_Cites: FR-18, FR-22, NFR-7_\n118\t\n119\t## Summary of new dependencies\n120\t\n121\tNone. Every requirement in this PRD (bulk transactional writes, capacity\n122\tchecks, uniqueness, admin UI, auth gating) is covered by Prisma 5,\n123\tPostgreSQL, MUI v9, and TypeScript already in the stack — intake's\n124\tconfidence-100% stack detection holds.\n125\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6adde39-f92b-490f-9ef1-b8393dd3c9ab","uuid":"86add27a-4652-41b2-90a5-86b75009d6fc","timestamp":"2026-10-27T16:30:17.873Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-05 Classes & Enrollment\n\nTicket: abcd-05 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Status: complete\n\nBase platform (from `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%):\nNext.js 14 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict, Prisma 5 / PostgreSQL,\nMUI v9 + emotion, `zod` for input validation, `date-fns`. This PRD is\nbrownfield on top of that stack — intake's detection notes already confirm\nit \"adds no new frontend framework or dependency,\" only new Prisma models\nand pages. The decisions below are the implementation-pattern deltas this\nPRD's requirements force, not a re-litigation of the base stack. No new\nruntime dependency is added by this PRD.\n\n## ADR-1: Bulk enroll/drop writes — `Prisma.$transaction` with batched\n`createMany`/`updateMany`, no job queue\n\n**Context.** NFR-3 requires adding 30 students to a section with 8 core\ncourses (240 resulting enrollments) to complete in under 1 second. FR-7–\nFR-10 describe enroll-on-add, drop-on-remove, move-section, and\ncreate-core-course as all-or-nothing operations touching many `Enrollment`\nrows at once.\n\n**Decision.** Implement each of these as a single `prisma.$transaction`\nthat computes the affected (studentId, courseId) pairs in memory first,\nthen issues one `enrollment.createMany({ skipDuplicates: true })` for new\nACTIVE rows and one `enrollment.updateMany` (keyed on the unique\n`[courseId, studentId]`) to reactivate previously-DROPPED rows, instead of\nlooping `create`/`update` calls per student per course. This is plain\nPrisma batch-write API already in the installed `@prisma/client` — no\nqueue, no background worker, no new dependency. 240 rows in one\n`createMany` plus one `updateMany` comfortably clears the 1-second bar on\nPostgres; a per-row loop (240 round-trips) would not.\n\n**Consequences.** Business logic that decides \"create vs. reactivate\" for\neach pair must be computed with a single prior `findMany`, not per-row\nqueries, to keep the whole operation at a small constant number of\nround-trips. If a future PRD needs enroll/drop across thousands of rows\n(bulk import), that's a new ADR for chunking or a background job — not a\nretrofit of this one.\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-3_\n\n## ADR-2: Capacity + uniqueness enforcement — DB unique constraints for\nidentity, in-transaction count check for capacity, no distributed lock\n\n**Context.** NFR-2 requires the one-section-per-student-per-year rule to be\na database constraint, not just app logic. FR-11 requires capacity\nenforcement (section and course) that refuses with CONFLICT unless an\nexplicit \"Override capacity\" flag is set, under concurrent admin actions.\n\n**Decision.** Identity/duplication rules (`@@unique([studentId,\nacademicYearId])` on `SectionMember`, `@@unique([courseId, studentId])` on\n`Enrollment`) are enforced by Postgres unique indexes already specified in\nFR-1; application code catches the Prisma `P2002` violation and maps it to\nthe existing CONFLICT error envelope rather than pre-checking existence\nfirst (avoids a check-then-insert race). Capacity, which is a *computed*\ncount rather than a stored invariant, is checked inside the same\n`$transaction` that performs the insert — `count()` the current\nmembers/enrollments, compare to `capacity`, and only then\n`create`/`createMany`, so a concurrent request either serializes behind the\ntransaction or re-checks and fails cleanly. No advisory lock, no Redis, no\ndistributed-lock library: Postgres's own transactional read-your-writes\nwithin one `$transaction` is sufficient at this app's scale (a school\nadmin console, not a high-concurrency storefront).\n\n**Consequences.** Capacity checks are \"good enough\" serialization, not a\nhard guarantee under extreme concurrent load from multiple admins hitting\nthe exact same section simultaneously; acceptable because this is an\ninternal admin tool with low write concurrency. If that ever changes,\nupgrading the capacity check to `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on a row is a small\nfollow-up, not a new ADR.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-11, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-3: No new dependency for the admin UI (tables, dialogs, pickers)\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 mandates every visible element in the sections/courses/\nenrollment screens come from `@mui/material`, `@mui/icons-material`, or MUI\nX, with no banned libraries (Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Radix, react-select,\netc.). FR-2–FR-6, FR-13–FR-19 describe filtered tables, create/edit\ndialogs, multi-select pickers, tabs, and card grids.\n\n**Decision.** Build all of it from the already-installed MUI v9 set plus\nthe existing shared components (`DataTable`, `UserPicker`, `ConfirmDialog`,\n`useToast`, `PageHeader`) per the brownfield reuse already confirmed in\nintake. Multi-select student/subject pickers extend `UserPicker`'s\nexisting `role` filter pattern rather than introducing a new\nautocomplete/select component. No new UI dependency.\n\n**Consequences.** None beyond the existing MUI-MCP lookup discipline\n(NFR-1) for any component variant not already used elsewhere in the\ncodebase.\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-6, FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-17, FR-19, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-4: Auth — keep the `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in, no new\nauth helper\n\n**Context.** NFR-7 flags that `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` and\n`requireUser()` described in the platform context don't exist yet — only\n`requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present. This is the same\nbrownfield gap PRD-04's architect handoff already flagged.\n\n**Decision.** New route handlers in this PRD (sections/courses/enrollment\nCRUD, move-section, roster) continue to use the existing\n`requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)`-style stand-in, and `assertCanViewCourse`/\n`assertCanManageCourse` (FR-18) are added as plain functions in\n`src/server/auth/course-access.ts` alongside the existing\n`student-access.ts`, not as part of a new `permissions.ts` module. No new\nauth library, no new abstraction invented ahead of PRD-01 landing it\nproperly.\n\n**Consequences.** Role-gating logic stays slightly duplicated across\nper-feature `*-access.ts` files until PRD-01 ships a real\n`permissions.ts`; that consolidation is PRD-01's job, not this one's.\n\n_Cites: FR-18, FR-22, NFR-7_\n\n## Summary of new dependencies\n\nNone. Every requirement in this PRD (bulk transactional writes, capacity\nchecks, uniqueness, admin UI, auth gating) is covered by Prisma 5,\nPostgreSQL, MUI v9, and TypeScript already in the stack — intake's\nconfidence-100% stack detection holds.\n","numLines":125,"startLine":1,"totalLines":125}}}
