ff-to-architect.json` lists it as a\n  blocker. Record the chosen approach (add `group` to `NavItem` vs. flat\n  entries) in `system-design.md`'s Overview per Compliance Rule 5.\n- **NFR-7's auth-helper gap is also a carried-forward blocker** — FR-18's\n  `course-access.ts` must be designed against today's `requireRole.ts`\n  stand-in, not the not-yet-built `permissions.ts`/`requireUser()`. Surface\n  this explicitly in `system-design.md`'s Overview so `dev-planning` doesn't\n  schedule work against auth helpers that don't exist.\n- **Two independent app-level uniqueness rules have no DB backstop and need\n  an explicit concurrency decision**: FR-3's \"one homeroom teacher per\n  section per year\" and FR-14's \"section required iff kind=CORE\" are both\n  service-layer validations only (unlike NFR-2's DB-constrained rule) —\n  `arch-design-system` should say where each is checked and whether a race\n  (two concurrent section edits) is acceptable risk or needs a DB-level\n  constraint too.\n- **NFR-4's roster scrubbing must be enforced at the API layer (FR-23),\n  not just the FR-17 UI tab** — call this out explicitly in the\n  traceability table so the response-shape rule isn't treated as\n  presentation-only.\n- **FR-5 and FR-10 share one course-creation-enrolls-members code path** —\n  bulk (checked subjects) vs. single (one course) are two entry points into\n  the same underlying function, same pattern as the enrollment engine note\n  above.\n","numLines":97,"startLine":1,"totalLines":97}}}
