{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0158h8CZxJX6WqjrnTJU9Q6D","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n2\t\n3\tTicket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete\n4\t\n5\tBase stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by\n6\t`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,\n7\tPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,\n8\tdate-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`\n9\tand `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below\n10\tonly cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force.\n11\t\n12\tBrownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):\n13\t- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in\n14\t  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,\n15\t  layout or route handler.\n16\t- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,\n17\t  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope\n18\t  changes are needed.\n19\t- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param.\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)\n24\t\n25\t**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT\n26\tsessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge\n27\truntime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load.\n28\t\n29\t**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:\n30\t- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,\n31\t  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape\n32\t  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.\n33\t- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,\n34\t  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the\n35\t  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).\n36\t- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.\n37\t- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34).\n38\t\n39\tDon't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and\n40\tthe adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD\n41\tdoesn't define.\n42\t\n43\t**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no\n44\tDB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which\n45\truns whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a\n46\tbeta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't\n47\tchange callback signatures mid-PRD.\n48\t\n49\t_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_\n50\t\n51\t## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`\n52\t\n53\t**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.\n54\tAuth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:\n55\t(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;\n56\t(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;\n57\t(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper.\n58\t\n59\t**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`\n60\tbecome `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return\n61\tshape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites\n62\tchanges from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a\n63\tmechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap\n64\t`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one\n65\tsession decode per request.\n66\t\n67\tOption (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk\n68\t(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an\n69\tasync read sync.\n70\t\n71\t**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has\n72\tno `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail\n73\ttypecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.\n74\tEnable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have\n75\tdev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,\n76\tuse `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;\n77\tin route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which\n78\t`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and\n79\tAPI paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and\n80\t`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified\n81\t(NFR-9).\n82\t\n83\t_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_\n84\t\n85\t## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users\n86\t\n87\t**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the\n88\tuser doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95.\n89\t\n90\t**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one\n91\tmodule-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is\n92\tmissing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use\n93\targon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt).\n94\t\n95\t**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,\n96\twhich leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the\n97\tfailedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one\n98\tsign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1).\n99\t\n100\t_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_\n101\t\n102\t## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes\n103\t\n104\t**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset\n105\tinvalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's\n106\tno session table.\n107\t\n108\t**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the\n109\tNode `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only\n110\t`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or\n111\tthe status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise\n112\tupdate `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`\n113\t(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.\n114\t\n115\t**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly\n116\taccepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through\n117\tfor page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call\n118\tthen catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).\n119\t\n120\t_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_\n121\t\n122\t## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)\n123\t\n124\t**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored\n125\thashes and constant-time comparison.\n126\t\n127\t**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\n128\tand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\n129\twith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n130\t\n131\t**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\n132\temailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n133\t\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\n134\tthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n135\t\n136\t_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n137\t\n138\t## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n139\t\n140\t**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\n141\tper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n142\t`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n143\t\n144\t**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\n145\tover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\n146\teach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\n147\tTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\n148\thelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\n149\troute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n150\t\n151\t**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\n152\taren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n153\t`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\n154\thorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\n155\tto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\n156\tlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n157\t\n158\t_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n159\t\n160\t## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n161\t\n162\t**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\n163\tFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n164\t\n165\t**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\n166\tpure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\n167\tchecklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The\n168\tcommon-password check needs the file at\n169\t`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read\n170\tthe file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client\n171\tchecklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the\n172\tcommon-password rule is reported from the server response.\n173\t\n174\t**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The\n175\tvalidator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`\n176\tread in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.\n177\t\n178\t_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_\n179\t\n180\t## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp\n181\t\n182\t**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to\n183\tbe looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth\n184\tpages and top-bar changes.\n185\t\n186\t**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:\n187\tCard, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),\n188\tAlert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top\n189\tbar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or\n190\tsonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the\n191\t`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session\n192\t(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't\n193\tstand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.\n194\t\n195\t**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level\n196\tAlert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.\n197\t\n198\t_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_\n199\t\n200\t## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration\n201\t\n202\t**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs\n203\t`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9\n204\tsays never rename.\n205\t\n206\t**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the\n207\texisting extra indexes. Add only\n208\t`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to\n209\tAuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing\n210\t(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them\n211\tin one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status\n212\t!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.\n213\t\n214\t**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and\n215\tnullable or defaulted columns only.\n216\t\n217\t_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_\n218\t\n219\t## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling\n220\t\n221\t**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the\n222\tlimiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the\n223\tauthorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use\n224\t`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for\n225\tsign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from\n226\tthe logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).\n227\t\n228\t_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_\n229\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8ecf6ce-8d48-4a6b-879b-0c956e2c759f","uuid":"0a9c1996-f1d4-4392-a0df-4640a18fa5f5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:36:11.419Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions & RBAC (SCH-001)\n\nTicket: abcd-1 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T00:00:00Z · Status: complete\n\nBase stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`, confidence 100%, confirmed by\n`package.json`): Next.js 14.2 App Router, React 18, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI v9 + emotion, zod 3, react-hook-form,\ndate-fns-tz. The auth dependencies are already installed: `next-auth ^5.0.0-beta.25`\nand `bcrypt ^6`. **This PRD adds no new runtime dependency.** The ADRs below\nonly cover the implementation choices this PRD's requirements force.\n\nBrownfield facts checked in the workspace (they correct intake in places):\n- `requireRole()` is sync and returns `{id, role}`. It has **50 call sites in\n  43 files**, not ~94. Every caller is already inside an `async` server page,\n  layout or route handler.\n- `src/server/http.ts` `ApiErrorCode` already includes `UNAUTHENTICATED` 401,\n  `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 and `INVALID_TOKEN` 410, so no envelope\n  changes are needed.\n- `AuditLog.ip String?` exists. `audit()` has no `request` param.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Auth.js v5 with a split config (edge-safe `auth.config.ts` + Node `auth.ts`)\n\n**Context.** FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12 and FR-34 need credential sign-in, JWT\nsessions and a `middleware.ts` gate. In Next 14, middleware runs on the Edge\nruntime, where `bcrypt` (a native addon) and Prisma's Node client can't load.\n\n**Decision.** Use the documented Auth.js v5 split:\n- `src/server/auth/auth.config.ts` (edge-safe): `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`,\n  `pages: { signIn: '/login' }`, the `authorized`/`jwt`/`session` shape\n  callbacks, and no providers that touch the DB.\n- `src/server/auth/auth.ts` (Node): `NextAuth({ ...authConfig, providers: [Credentials({ authorize })] })`,\n  exporting `{ handlers, auth, signIn, signOut }`. It also holds the\n  DB-touching `jwt` callback logic (FR-11).\n- `middleware.ts` builds `NextAuth(authConfig).auth`. It only decodes the JWT.\n- `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` re-exports `handlers` (FR-34).\n\nDon't use a Prisma adapter. JWT sessions with Credentials don't need one, and\nthe adapter would require Account/Session/VerificationToken models the PRD\ndoesn't define.\n\n**Consequences.** Middleware can't check `passwordChangedAt` because it has no\nDB access. That check lives in the Node-side `jwt` callback (ADR-4), which\nruns whenever `auth()` is called from server code. Because next-auth is a\nbeta, pin the installed version exactly (drop the `^`) so a beta bump can't\nchange callback signatures mid-PRD.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-4, FR-10, FR-12, FR-34, NFR-2_\n\n## ADR-2: `requireRole()` / `requireUser()` become async; update all 50 call sites with `await`\n\n**Context.** FR-28 and FR-29 replace `PLACEHOLDER_USER` with the real session.\nAuth.js `auth()` is async. Intake flagged this as a blocker. Options:\n(a) make the helpers async and add `await` at each call site;\n(b) keep them sync by reading identity from headers set by middleware;\n(c) use React `cache()` plus a sync wrapper.\n\n**Decision.** (a). `getCurrentUser()`, `requireUser()` and `requireRole(...roles)`\nbecome `async` with the same arguments and the same `{id, role, ...}` return\nshape, so the signature stays compatible (FR-29). Each of the 50 call sites\nchanges from `requireRole(...)` to `await requireRole(...)`. That's a\nmechanical one-token edit, and every caller is already async. Wrap\n`getCurrentUser` in React `cache()` so a page plus its layout share one\nsession decode per request.\n\nOption (b) was rejected: identity read from headers is a spoofing risk\n(NFR-7), and it skips the FR-11 revocation check. Option (c) can't make an\nasync read sync.\n\n**Consequences.** `tsc` catches every missed `await`, because a `Promise` has\nno `.role` or `.id`. Bare `requireRole('ADMIN');` statements don't fail\ntypecheck, though, and a forgotten `await` there silently does nothing.\nEnable `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises` for `src/**`, or have\ndev-planning grep for un-awaited calls. Redirect-vs-throw: in server pages,\nuse `redirect('/login')` / `redirect('/forbidden')` from `next/navigation`;\nin route handlers, throw `AppError` (UNAUTHENTICATED / FORBIDDEN), which\n`withHandler` already maps. Implement this as one helper that the page and\nAPI paths both call, not two copies. `course-access.ts` and\n`student-access.ts` keep taking the resolved user object and aren't modified\n(NFR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-28, FR-29, NFR-7, NFR-8, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-3: Password hashing with the existing `bcrypt` at cost 12, plus a dummy compare for unknown users\n\n**Context.** FR-4 sets cost 12. NFR-3 requires constant-ish timing when the\nuser doesn't exist. NFR-4 requires sign-in in under 1 s at p95.\n\n**Decision.** Use `bcrypt.hash(pw, 12)` / `bcrypt.compare`. Precompute one\nmodule-level dummy hash at cost 12 and compare against it when the user is\nmissing, disabled or locked, so every path pays one bcrypt compare. Don't use\nargon2 (it's a new native dependency and the PRD specifies bcrypt).\n\n**Consequences.** One cost-12 compare takes about 250 ms on modern hardware,\nwhich leaves room under the 1 s budget for the user lookup, the\nfailedLoginCount update and the audit insert. Never call bcrypt twice on one\nsign-in path. bcrypt runs only in Node code, never in middleware (ADR-1).\n\n_Cites: FR-4, FR-6, NFR-3, NFR-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Session revocation via a `passwordChangedAt` check in the jwt callback, throttled to every 5 minutes\n\n**Context.** FR-11 and FR-23 require that a password change or reset\ninvalidates other sessions. Sessions are stateless JWTs (FR-10), so there's\nno session table.\n\n**Decision.** Store `iat` and a `checkedAt` timestamp in the token. In the\nNode `jwt` callback, if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, select only\n`passwordChangedAt, status` for `token.id`. If `passwordChangedAt > iat` or\nthe status isn't ACTIVE, return `null` to invalidate the session. Otherwise\nupdate `checkedAt`. For FR-23's \"re-issue current session\", call `unstable_update()`\n(the v5 `update`) after the change, which mints a token with a fresh `iat`.\n\n**Consequences.** Revocation can lag by up to 5 minutes. The PRD explicitly\naccepts that window. Edge middleware can still let a revoked token through\nfor page navigation in that window. The server-side `requireUser()` call\nthen catches it, and that call is the real guard (NFR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-10, FR-11, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-7_\n\n## ADR-5: Tokens with `node:crypto` only (randomBytes, SHA-256, timingSafeEqual)\n\n**Context.** FR-13 needs a single AuthToken module. NFR-1 requires stored\nhashes and constant-time comparison.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` uses `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`\nand `createHash('sha256')`. Look up by the hash (the DB index), then confirm\nwith `timingSafeEqual` on the hash buffers. No JWT, nanoid or other library.\n\n**Consequences.** Raw tokens exist only in the returned value and the\nemailed or logged link, never in the DB or audit metadata (FR-31). The\n\"invalidate older unused tokens\" step (FR-20, FR-26) is one `updateMany`\nthat sets `usedAt` in the same `$transaction` as the create.\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-20, FR-26, FR-31, NFR-1_\n\n## ADR-6: In-memory sliding-window rate limiter (a `Map` of timestamp arrays); no Redis\n\n**Context.** FR-7 (10 per IP per 5 min) and FR-19 (3 per email per hour, 10\nper IP per hour) explicitly ask for an exported in-memory limiter in\n`src/server/rate-limit.ts`. The PRD adds no infrastructure (NFR-11).\n\n**Decision.** One `rateLimit(key, limit, windowMs): { ok, retryAfterMs }`\nover a module-level `Map<string, number[]>`, pruning expired timestamps on\neach call. Namespace keys like `login:ip:<ip>` and `forgot:email:<email>`.\nTake the IP from the first entry of `x-forwarded-for`, using the same\nhelper `audit()` uses (FR-30). Call it from the Node side (authorize and\nroute handlers), not middleware, because Edge isolates don't share memory.\n\n**Consequences.** The ceiling is per process: limits reset on restart and\naren't shared across instances. Mark the code with a\n`ponytail: per-process limiter, swap the Map for Redis/Postgres if\nhorizontally scaled` comment. With Credentials, a RATE_LIMITED outcome has\nto go through Auth.js's `CredentialsSignin` subclass `code` to reach the\nlogin form (FR-5, FR-7).\n\n_Cites: FR-7, FR-19, FR-30, NFR-8, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-7: Validation with the existing zod + react-hook-form; one policy schema shared by client and server\n\n**Context.** FR-17 defines the policy. FR-18 needs a live checklist.\nFR-16, FR-21 and FR-23 must enforce the policy server-side.\n\n**Decision.** `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` exports the zod schema plus a\npure `passwordChecks(pw, email)` that returns per-rule booleans. The\nchecklist UI and the zod `superRefine` both use that function. The\ncommon-password check needs the file at\n`src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt`, so it runs only on the server: read\nthe file once into a `Set` with `fs` in a server-only module. The client\nchecklist shows the length, letter, digit and not-email rules live, and the\ncommon-password rule is reported from the server response.\n\n**Consequences.** The 1,000-word list never ships in the client bundle. The\nvalidator file has to stay importable from the client, so keep the `fs`\nread in a separate server-only file that adds the common-password refinement.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-18, FR-16, FR-21, FR-23, NFR-5, NFR-8_\n\n## ADR-8: UI with MUI v9 only (Sign-in template patterns), documented through mui-mcp\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 bans non-MUI UI libraries and requires every component to\nbe looked up through mui-mcp. FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24 and FR-32 add auth\npages and top-bar changes.\n\n**Decision.** Use the installed `@mui/material` / `@mui/icons-material` 9.5:\nCard, TextField, Button, InputAdornment + IconButton (Visibility icons),\nAlert, List/ListItemIcon for the checklist, and Menu/Avatar/Chip in the top\nbar. Use the existing `useToast` for toasts (FR-21, FR-23), not notistack or\nsonner. Auth pages live in a `src/app/(auth)` route group outside the\n`(app)` shell. The mui-mcp server was reachable from this session\n(`useMuiDocs` / `fetchDocs` tools were listed), so the NFR-6 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still check it itself.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. `aria-live=\"polite\"` on the form-level\nAlert and focus-first-invalid (via react-hook-form `setFocus`) meet NFR-5.\n\n_Cites: FR-3, FR-15, FR-18, FR-24, FR-32, FR-33, NFR-5, NFR-6_\n\n## ADR-9: Schema reconciled additively with one Prisma migration\n\n**Context.** FR-1 and the intake blockers list the divergences: `ip` vs\n`ipAddress`, the extra `ARCHIVED` value, different AuditLog indexes. NFR-9\nsays never rename.\n\n**Decision.** Keep `AuditLog.ip`, keep `UserStatus.ARCHIVED` and keep the\nexisting extra indexes. Add only\n`@@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])` to\nAuditLog, plus any User auth fields the diff finds missing\n(failedLoginCount, lockedUntil, lastLoginAt, passwordChangedAt). Apply them\nin one `prisma migrate dev --name auth_reconcile` migration. FR-4's \"status\n!= ACTIVE\" check already rejects ARCHIVED.\n\n**Consequences.** The migration has no destructive steps. It adds indexes and\nnullable or defaulted columns only.\n\n_Cites: FR-1, FR-2, FR-4, FR-6, NFR-9_\n\n## ADR-10: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; no new test tooling\n\n**Decision.** Run unit and integration tests (FR-36) in `vitest`, calling the\nlimiter, tokens, policy and callbackUrl modules as pure functions. Test the\nauthorize function and the service functions against the test DB. Use\n`@playwright/test` (with the installed `@axe-core/playwright` for NFR-5) for\nsign-in/out, the failure message and forgot→reset. The reset link comes from\nthe logged `sendAuthEmail` output (FR-22).\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-36, NFR-5, NFR-10_\n","numLines":229,"startLine":1,"totalLines":229}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0158h8CZxJX6WqjrnTJU9Q6D","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghRb9kqvUCQFeKgWN4ba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VtHpJyfHxWMg4hyMVhfC1o","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10742,"cache_read_input_tokens":26153,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10742},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8ecf6ce-8d48-4a6b-879b-0c956e2c759f","uuid":"a91ea57b-b762-4fa1-b697-31a88ba4626b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:36:11.936Z","request_id":"req_011CghRb9XCy2beGFqYCorJK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VtHpJyfHxWMg4hyMVhfC1o":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHpJyfHxWMg4hyMVhfC1o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-1\n2\t\n3\tSupporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.\n4\tSource: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions\n5\t& RBAC; 37 FR + 12 NFR = 49 requirements, 94% confidence, 4 blockers in\n6\t`handoff-to-architect.json`). Run mode: `--brownfield --tech-stack react`.\n7\t\n8\tArchitectural concern buckets: **Data Model**, **Auth Core (Auth.js)**,\n9\t**Credential Verification**, **Lockout & Rate Limiting**, **Session/JWT**,\n10\t**Edge Middleware**, **Token Module**, **Invite Flow**, **Password Policy**,\n11\t**Password Reset Flow**, **Change Password**, **Email Stub**, **Users\n12\tService**, **Authorization Helpers**, **Audit**, **App Shell & Nav**, **Auth\n13\tUI Surface**, **REST API Surface**, **Seed**, **Test Harness**, **Security**,\n14\t**Performance**, **Accessibility**, **Design System (MUI-only)**, **Scope\n15\tDiscipline**.\n16\t\n17\t## Brownfield facts verified on disk (correct intake where it differs)\n18\t\n19\t- `requireRole(` is called at **~48 sites in ~42 files**, not ~94 as intake\n20\t  says. Every one of those files already contains `async`, i.e. the callers\n21\t  are async route handlers / server components. **Making `requireRole()`\n22\t  async (callers add `await`) is a mechanical change, not a redesign.**\n23\t  `src/server/auth/course-access.ts` consumes requireRole's result but must\n24\t  not be modified (NFR-9) — keep the returned shape a superset of\n25\t  `{ id, role }`.\n26\t- `src/server/http.ts` already defines `AppError` codes `UNAUTHENTICATED`\n27\t  401, `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429, `CONFLICT` 409, plus Prisma\n28\t  P2002 → CONFLICT mapping. No new error infra needed.\n29\t- `src/server/audit.ts` has `audit(input)` that swallows errors; no `request`\n30\t  param, never writes `ip`. Nothing in `src` reads `x-forwarded-for` today.\n31\t- `src/lib/logger.ts`, `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts`,\n32\t  `src/config/navigation.ts` exist. `src/app/forbidden` exists (target for\n33\t  FR-29 redirect). `src/app/(public)` has `layout.tsx` + `page.tsx` only.\n34\t- `src/app/api/health` exists; `src/app/api/auth` does not.\n35\t- `users.ts` exports `createUser`, `setUserStatus(actorId, userId, status)`,\n36\t  `unlockUser(userId)`, `sendInvite(userId): Promise<{id, inviteSentAt}>`.\n37\t\n38\t## Functional Requirements\n39\t\n40\t| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n41\t|----|------------------------|-------|\n42\t| FR-1 | Data Model | Additive Prisma migration only: add `AuditLog @@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])`. Keep `ip` column, `ARCHIVED` enum value, and existing extra indexes (resolves blocker 1 per intake defaults). Verify `User` has `passwordHash`, `failedLoginCount`, `lockedUntil`, `lastLoginAt`, `passwordChangedAt`, `archivedAt`; add any missing as nullable/defaulted columns. |\n43\t| FR-2 | Data Model / Credential Verification | Normalise `email.trim().toLowerCase()` in one place (Zod schema transform in `validators/auth.ts`) used by authorize, forgot-password and `createUser`. DB-level backstop optional (Postgres `citext` would be a type change — avoid; app-level is enough). |\n44\t| FR-3 | Auth UI Surface | `src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx` — new `(auth)` route group with its own unauthenticated layout (no AppShell). Client component calling `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` so error codes can map to FR-5 messages. |\n45\t| FR-4 | Credential Verification | Lives in Auth.js `Credentials.authorize`, but logic should be a plain service fn (`src/server/auth/credentials.ts` or inside the Auth.js config file) so integration tests (FR-36) can call it without HTTP. Order: rate-limit → lookup → status/archived/locked checks → bcrypt compare (cost 12). Runs on the **Node runtime** (bcrypt is native; not edge-safe). |\n46\t| FR-5 | Credential Verification / Auth UI | Auth.js v5 lets `authorize` throw a `CredentialsSignin` subclass with a `code` (`invalid`, `locked`, `disabled`, `rate_limited`). The UI maps code → message. Locked message needs remaining minutes from `lockedUntil` → the code must carry minutes (e.g. `locked:7`) or the UI calls back. Design must pick one. |\n47\t| FR-6 | Lockout & Rate Limiting | Per-user counter on `User` row. Increment + lock must be one atomic update (`update ... failedLoginCount: { increment: 1 }` then conditional lock) to avoid races under parallel attempts. Pure helper `computeLockout(count, now)` for unit test. |\n48\t| FR-7 | Lockout & Rate Limiting | `src/server/rate-limit.ts`: in-memory sliding window keyed by string (`login:ip:<ip>`). Single-process only — acceptable per PRD; note ceiling (multi-instance needs Redis). Reused by FR-19 with different keys/limits, so API is generic `check(key, limit, windowMs)`. Needs client IP → shared `getClientIp(request)` helper (also FR-30). |\n49\t| FR-8 | Auth Core | Pure `safeCallbackUrl(url): string` — accept only paths starting with `/` and not `//` or `/\\`; else `/dashboard`. Used by login page and Auth.js `redirect` callback. Unit-tested (FR-36). |\n50\t| FR-9 | Audit | Called from authorize branches. `auth.login.failure` metadata = `{ email, reason }` only (FR-31). `actorId` = user id when known, null for unknown email. |\n51\t| FR-10 | Session/JWT | Auth.js `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`. `jwt`/`session` callbacks copy `id, role, firstName, lastName, email`. Module augmentation of `next-auth` types for typed `session.user`. |\n52\t| FR-11 | Session/JWT | `jwt` callback stores `checkedAt` on the token; if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, DB read of `passwordChangedAt`/`status`; return `null` to invalidate. This DB hit means the jwt callback must not run on edge → middleware must use an edge-safe config split (see FR-12). |\n53\t| FR-12 | Edge Middleware | `middleware.ts` runs on edge: **cannot import Prisma or bcrypt**. Standard Auth.js v5 pattern: `auth.config.ts` (edge-safe, no providers' DB code) used by middleware; `auth.ts` (full, Node) for handlers. Middleware only checks token presence/validity; FR-11 revalidation happens in Node. Matcher excludes `_next`, static, `/api/auth/*`, `/api/health`, `(auth)` pages, `(public)` pages, plus `src/config/public-routes.ts` list. Pages → redirect `/login?callbackUrl=`; `/api/*` → 401 JSON envelope. |\n54\t| FR-13 | Token Module | `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` — the only writer/reader of `AuthToken`. `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`, store `sha256` hex. Lookup by hash (unique index) → constant-time compare after fetch (NFR-1). `consumeToken` should be conditional (`updateMany where usedAt null`) so double-submit can't consume twice. Add `invalidateUnused(userId, type)` used by FR-20/FR-26. |\n55\t| FR-14 | Invite Flow | `src/app/(auth)/invite/[token]/page.tsx` server component calls `verifyToken(raw, 'INVITE')`; invalid → error Alert. Token is in the URL path → ensure it isn't logged by request logging. |\n56\t| FR-15 | Invite Flow / Auth UI | Read-only name/email from verified token's user; password + confirm fields reuse the FR-18 checklist component. |\n57\t| FR-16 | Invite Flow / REST API | `POST /api/auth/accept-invite` — **note path sits under `/api/auth/*` alongside the `[...nextauth]` catch-all**; Next.js static segments win over catch-alls so this works, but design must confirm. Service in a transaction: verify → policy check → hash → `status=ACTIVE`, `passwordChangedAt=now` → consume. \"Signs the user in\": simplest is client calls `signIn('credentials')` with the just-set password after 200 OK (server-side session minting outside Auth.js is fragile). Audit `auth.invite.accepted`. |\n58\t| FR-17 | Password Policy | `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` — Zod schema shared client+server. Common-password list lives in `src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt` (server-only file read) → split: client checks length/letter/digit/≠email; server additionally checks common list. FR-18 checklist can't show the common-list rule live without shipping the list; either ship the list (~8 KB) to client or show that rule only on submit. Design decision. |\n59\t| FR-18 | Password Policy / Auth UI | Shared `PasswordChecklist` component (MUI List + icons) used by invite, reset and change-password pages. |\n60\t| FR-19 | Password Reset Flow / Lockout & Rate Limiting | `POST /api/auth/forgot-password` — identical response body and similar timing whether or not user exists (NFR-3 spirit). Two rate-limit keys: `forgot:email:<email>` (3/h), `forgot:ip:<ip>` (10/h). Over limit: still the generic message, or RATE_LIMITED 429? PRD says \"always responds\" → design must decide; 429 on IP limit is safe, email limit should stay silent to avoid enumeration. |\n61\t| FR-20 | Password Reset Flow | Only ACTIVE users get a token; `invalidateUnused(userId,'PASSWORD_RESET')` then `createToken(…, 60 min)` then `sendAuthEmail`. Unknown email → no DB write. |\n62\t| FR-21 | Password Reset Flow | `POST /api/auth/reset-password` transaction: verify → policy → hash → `passwordChangedAt=now`, `failedLoginCount=0`, `lockedUntil=null` → consume. Audit `auth.password.reset`. Success toast after redirect needs cross-navigation signal (`/login?reset=1` query param read on login page → `useToast`). |\n63\t| FR-22 | Email Stub | `src/server/auth/email.ts` `sendAuthEmail(user, kind, link)` → `logger.info`. Single seam PRD-04 swaps for EmailOutbox. Link base URL from env (`AUTH_URL`/`NEXTAUTH_URL`). |\n64\t| FR-23 | Change Password | `/account/security` page inside `(app)` (authenticated). `POST /api/account/password` → `requireUser()` → bcrypt compare current → policy → hash → `passwordChangedAt=now`. \"Re-issue current session\": after `passwordChangedAt` bump, current JWT's `iat` < `passwordChangedAt` would be invalidated by FR-11 → must refresh current token (client `update()` from `useSession`, with jwt callback trigger `update` resetting `iat`/`checkedAt`). Key subtlety for design. Wrong current password → 400 with field-level `details` for the form. |\n65\t| FR-24 | App Shell & Nav | Add menu item in `shell.tsx` user menu → `/account/security`. All roles. |\n66\t| FR-25 | Users Service | No new `createUser`; confirm P2002 on email → CONFLICT via existing `http.ts` mapping. Keep `setUserStatus(actorId, userId, status)` and `unlockUser(userId)` (resolves blocker 2 per intake defaults). Ensure `createUser` lower-cases email (FR-2). |\n67\t| FR-26 | Users Service / Token Module | `createInviteToken(userId)` in `users.ts` → `tokens.invalidateUnused` + `tokens.createToken(userId,'INVITE',{days:7})`. |\n68\t| FR-27 | Users Service / Email Stub | `sendInvite` body: `createInviteToken` → `sendAuthEmail(user,'invite',link)`; keep return `{id, inviteSentAt}` for existing callers. |\n69\t| FR-28 | Authorization Helpers | `requireRole.ts`: delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER`; add `getCurrentUser()` (wraps `auth()`, memoised per request with React `cache()`), `requireUser()`. Server pages vs route handlers need different failure behaviour (redirect vs throw). Cleanest: separate fns or an option, since there's no reliable runtime way to detect context. Resolves blocker 3: **go async**; ~48 call sites add `await` (all already in async fns). No `permissions.ts`. |\n70\t| FR-29 | Authorization Helpers | `requireRole(...roles)` → `Promise<SessionUser>` (superset of `{id, role}` so `course-access.ts` compiles unchanged). Throw `AppError('FORBIDDEN')` in handlers; pages redirect `/forbidden` (route exists). No `can()`. |\n71\t| FR-30 | Audit | Add optional `request?: Request` to `AuditInput`; `ip = getClientIp(request)` (first value of `x-forwarded-for`, trimmed). Keep inside try/catch. Shared helper with FR-7. |\n72\t| FR-31 | Audit / Security | Enforce by construction: audit callers pass explicit metadata objects; optionally a small denylist strip (`password`, `passwordHash`, `token`) in `audit()` as defence-in-depth. Covered by test. |\n73\t| FR-32 | App Shell & Nav | `shell.tsx` reads session — either the `(app)/layout.tsx` server component passes `getCurrentUser()` as a prop, or `SessionProvider` + `useSession`. Prop-passing avoids a client fetch and flash. Sign out → `signOut({ redirectTo: '/login' })`. Role badge + initials via MUI `Chip`/`Avatar`. |\n74\t| FR-33 | App Shell & Nav | Filter `navigation.ts` entries by role (server-side, before passing to shell). UX only — NFR-7 still applies on every page. |\n75\t| FR-34 | Auth Core / REST API | `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` exporting `{ GET, POST } = handlers` from `auth.ts`. |\n76\t| FR-35 | Seed | Extend `prisma/seed.ts` idempotently (`upsert` by email): 6 ACTIVE users (bcrypt cost 12 of `SchoolHub123!`) + `invited@` INVITED with invite created via `createInviteToken` and link logged. Keep `office@`. Seeding goes through `tokens.ts` (FR-13 single-module rule). |\n77\t| FR-36 | Test Harness | Unit (pure fns: policy, token hash/expiry, `computeLockout`, rate limiter, `safeCallbackUrl`) → design should keep these logic pieces as pure exported fns. Integration against DB for authorize/accept/reset/change. Playwright needs seeded users (FR-35) and the reset link from logs → e2e must read the token from DB or a test hook rather than email. |\n78\t| FR-37 | Scope Discipline / Auth Core | No `/signup` route and no sign-up endpoint; the only path to ACTIVE is FR-16 (or admin `setUserStatus`). Middleware public list must not include any sign-up path. |\n79\t\n80\t## Non-Functional Requirements\n81\t\n82\t| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n83\t|----|------------------------|-------|\n84\t| NFR-1 | Security / Token Module | SHA-256 at rest; `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers. Unique index on `AuthToken.tokenHash` makes lookup O(1). |\n85\t| NFR-2 | Security / Auth Core | Auth.js defaults already give HttpOnly, `__Secure-` prefix + Secure in prod, SameSite=Lax, CSRF on its own endpoints. **Custom POST routes (`accept-invite`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `account/password`) are outside Auth.js CSRF** — design must cover them (Origin/`Sec-Fetch-Site` same-origin check in route or middleware; SameSite=Lax alone doesn't protect unauthenticated endpoints). |\n86\t| NFR-3 | Security / Credential Verification | Precompute a dummy bcrypt hash at module load; compare against it when the user is missing so timing matches. Same idea for forgot-password (no branch-visible timing difference). |\n87\t| NFR-4 | Performance | bcrypt cost 12 ≈ 250 ms; budget fine. Keep authorize to 1 user query + 1 update + async audit; don't await non-critical writes serially beyond that. |\n88\t| NFR-5 | Accessibility | Form `Alert` inside `aria-live=\"polite\"` region; on submit-with-errors focus first invalid `TextField` (`inputRef`). Shared form-error pattern across 5 auth forms. |\n89\t| NFR-6 | Design System (MUI-only) | All auth pages, checklist, shell menu changes use MUI v9 only; dev must look up each component via mui-mcp. mui-mcp tools are visible in this environment (`useMuiDocs`, `fetchDocs`) — blocker 4 currently looks unblocked, dev must still confirm. |\n90\t| NFR-7 | Authorization Helpers | Every new route handler and `(app)` page calls `requireUser()`/`requireRole()`; middleware is a first gate, not the only one. |\n91\t| NFR-8 | REST API Surface | All custom auth routes: Zod parse → service → `{data}` / `{error:{code,message,details}}` using existing `http.ts` helpers. Auth.js catch-all is the sole exception (its own protocol). |\n92\t| NFR-9 | Scope Discipline | Reuse `audit.ts`, `users.ts`, `requireRole.ts`, `useToast`, `AppError`; no renames; `course-access.ts` / `student-access.ts` untouched (constrains FR-29 return type). |\n93\t| NFR-10 | Test Harness | Lint/typecheck/test green — the async `requireRole` change (FR-28/29) touches ~42 files, so typecheck is the safety net. `logger` only; `console.log` banned except the dev email transport. |\n94\t| NFR-11 | Scope Discipline | No admin UI, SSO, MFA, magic links, or email infra. |\n95\t| NFR-12 | Data Model / Auth UI | Timestamps UTC in DB. Lockout \"N minutes\" is a duration (timezone-independent); `lastLoginAt` display formatted in `SchoolSettings.timezone` (existing settings model). |\n96\t\n97\t## Cross-cutting items for `arch-design-system`\n98\t\n99\t1. **Edge/Node split** (FR-4, FR-11, FR-12): `auth.config.ts` edge-safe for middleware; `auth.ts` Node with Prisma/bcrypt.\n100\t2. **requireRole goes async** (FR-28/29, blocker 3): ~48 sites, all already in async fns; return type superset of `{id, role}`.\n101\t3. **Session re-issue after password change** (FR-11 vs FR-23): current session must survive its own `passwordChangedAt` bump.\n102\t4. **CSRF for custom auth POSTs** (NFR-2): not covered by Auth.js.\n103\t5. **Shared `getClientIp(request)`** (FR-7, FR-19, FR-30).\n104\t6. **Shared password UI + Zod schema** (FR-15/17/18/21/23); decide whether the common-password list ships to the client.\n105\t7. **Rate limiter is per-process** (FR-7/FR-19): fine for this PRD, Redis if multi-instance.\n106\t8. **Additive migration only** (FR-1, blocker 1): two AuditLog indexes plus any missing User columns.\n107\t\n108\t## Blockers carried forward from intake\n109\t\n110\t- FR-1 schema divergences → resolved by additive migration (above).\n111\t- FR-25 signature mismatch → keep the existing signatures.\n112\t- FR-28/29 sync vs async → recommend async (call sites are fewer than intake reported and are all already async).\n113\t- NFR-6 mui-mcp reachability → tools are registered in this environment; dev must still check before writing UI.\n114\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8ecf6ce-8d48-4a6b-879b-0c956e2c759f","uuid":"c02718b4-f6e9-4c81-a3e5-f65c70e2e9e4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:36:11.958Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md","content":"# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-1\n\nSupporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.\nSource: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (PRD-01 Authentication, Sessions\n& RBAC; 37 FR + 12 NFR = 49 requirements, 94% confidence, 4 blockers in\n`handoff-to-architect.json`). Run mode: `--brownfield --tech-stack react`.\n\nArchitectural concern buckets: **Data Model**, **Auth Core (Auth.js)**,\n**Credential Verification**, **Lockout & Rate Limiting**, **Session/JWT**,\n**Edge Middleware**, **Token Module**, **Invite Flow**, **Password Policy**,\n**Password Reset Flow**, **Change Password**, **Email Stub**, **Users\nService**, **Authorization Helpers**, **Audit**, **App Shell & Nav**, **Auth\nUI Surface**, **REST API Surface**, **Seed**, **Test Harness**, **Security**,\n**Performance**, **Accessibility**, **Design System (MUI-only)**, **Scope\nDiscipline**.\n\n## Brownfield facts verified on disk (correct intake where it differs)\n\n- `requireRole(` is called at **~48 sites in ~42 files**, not ~94 as intake\n  says. Every one of those files already contains `async`, i.e. the callers\n  are async route handlers / server components. **Making `requireRole()`\n  async (callers add `await`) is a mechanical change, not a redesign.**\n  `src/server/auth/course-access.ts` consumes requireRole's result but must\n  not be modified (NFR-9) — keep the returned shape a superset of\n  `{ id, role }`.\n- `src/server/http.ts` already defines `AppError` codes `UNAUTHENTICATED`\n  401, `FORBIDDEN` 403, `RATE_LIMITED` 429, `CONFLICT` 409, plus Prisma\n  P2002 → CONFLICT mapping. No new error infra needed.\n- `src/server/audit.ts` has `audit(input)` that swallows errors; no `request`\n  param, never writes `ip`. Nothing in `src` reads `x-forwarded-for` today.\n- `src/lib/logger.ts`, `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts`,\n  `src/config/navigation.ts` exist. `src/app/forbidden` exists (target for\n  FR-29 redirect). `src/app/(public)` has `layout.tsx` + `page.tsx` only.\n- `src/app/api/health` exists; `src/app/api/auth` does not.\n- `users.ts` exports `createUser`, `setUserStatus(actorId, userId, status)`,\n  `unlockUser(userId)`, `sendInvite(userId): Promise<{id, inviteSentAt}>`.\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| FR-1 | Data Model | Additive Prisma migration only: add `AuditLog @@index([entityType, entityId])` and `@@index([actorId, createdAt])`. Keep `ip` column, `ARCHIVED` enum value, and existing extra indexes (resolves blocker 1 per intake defaults). Verify `User` has `passwordHash`, `failedLoginCount`, `lockedUntil`, `lastLoginAt`, `passwordChangedAt`, `archivedAt`; add any missing as nullable/defaulted columns. |\n| FR-2 | Data Model / Credential Verification | Normalise `email.trim().toLowerCase()` in one place (Zod schema transform in `validators/auth.ts`) used by authorize, forgot-password and `createUser`. DB-level backstop optional (Postgres `citext` would be a type change — avoid; app-level is enough). |\n| FR-3 | Auth UI Surface | `src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx` — new `(auth)` route group with its own unauthenticated layout (no AppShell). Client component calling `signIn('credentials', { redirect: false })` so error codes can map to FR-5 messages. |\n| FR-4 | Credential Verification | Lives in Auth.js `Credentials.authorize`, but logic should be a plain service fn (`src/server/auth/credentials.ts` or inside the Auth.js config file) so integration tests (FR-36) can call it without HTTP. Order: rate-limit → lookup → status/archived/locked checks → bcrypt compare (cost 12). Runs on the **Node runtime** (bcrypt is native; not edge-safe). |\n| FR-5 | Credential Verification / Auth UI | Auth.js v5 lets `authorize` throw a `CredentialsSignin` subclass with a `code` (`invalid`, `locked`, `disabled`, `rate_limited`). The UI maps code → message. Locked message needs remaining minutes from `lockedUntil` → the code must carry minutes (e.g. `locked:7`) or the UI calls back. Design must pick one. |\n| FR-6 | Lockout & Rate Limiting | Per-user counter on `User` row. Increment + lock must be one atomic update (`update ... failedLoginCount: { increment: 1 }` then conditional lock) to avoid races under parallel attempts. Pure helper `computeLockout(count, now)` for unit test. |\n| FR-7 | Lockout & Rate Limiting | `src/server/rate-limit.ts`: in-memory sliding window keyed by string (`login:ip:<ip>`). Single-process only — acceptable per PRD; note ceiling (multi-instance needs Redis). Reused by FR-19 with different keys/limits, so API is generic `check(key, limit, windowMs)`. Needs client IP → shared `getClientIp(request)` helper (also FR-30). |\n| FR-8 | Auth Core | Pure `safeCallbackUrl(url): string` — accept only paths starting with `/` and not `//` or `/\\`; else `/dashboard`. Used by login page and Auth.js `redirect` callback. Unit-tested (FR-36). |\n| FR-9 | Audit | Called from authorize branches. `auth.login.failure` metadata = `{ email, reason }` only (FR-31). `actorId` = user id when known, null for unknown email. |\n| FR-10 | Session/JWT | Auth.js `session: { strategy: 'jwt', maxAge: 8h, updateAge }`. `jwt`/`session` callbacks copy `id, role, firstName, lastName, email`. Module augmentation of `next-auth` types for typed `session.user`. |\n| FR-11 | Session/JWT | `jwt` callback stores `checkedAt` on the token; if `now - checkedAt > 5 min`, DB read of `passwordChangedAt`/`status`; return `null` to invalidate. This DB hit means the jwt callback must not run on edge → middleware must use an edge-safe config split (see FR-12). |\n| FR-12 | Edge Middleware | `middleware.ts` runs on edge: **cannot import Prisma or bcrypt**. Standard Auth.js v5 pattern: `auth.config.ts` (edge-safe, no providers' DB code) used by middleware; `auth.ts` (full, Node) for handlers. Middleware only checks token presence/validity; FR-11 revalidation happens in Node. Matcher excludes `_next`, static, `/api/auth/*`, `/api/health`, `(auth)` pages, `(public)` pages, plus `src/config/public-routes.ts` list. Pages → redirect `/login?callbackUrl=`; `/api/*` → 401 JSON envelope. |\n| FR-13 | Token Module | `src/server/auth/tokens.ts` — the only writer/reader of `AuthToken`. `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64url')`, store `sha256` hex. Lookup by hash (unique index) → constant-time compare after fetch (NFR-1). `consumeToken` should be conditional (`updateMany where usedAt null`) so double-submit can't consume twice. Add `invalidateUnused(userId, type)` used by FR-20/FR-26. |\n| FR-14 | Invite Flow | `src/app/(auth)/invite/[token]/page.tsx` server component calls `verifyToken(raw, 'INVITE')`; invalid → error Alert. Token is in the URL path → ensure it isn't logged by request logging. |\n| FR-15 | Invite Flow / Auth UI | Read-only name/email from verified token's user; password + confirm fields reuse the FR-18 checklist component. |\n| FR-16 | Invite Flow / REST API | `POST /api/auth/accept-invite` — **note path sits under `/api/auth/*` alongside the `[...nextauth]` catch-all**; Next.js static segments win over catch-alls so this works, but design must confirm. Service in a transaction: verify → policy check → hash → `status=ACTIVE`, `passwordChangedAt=now` → consume. \"Signs the user in\": simplest is client calls `signIn('credentials')` with the just-set password after 200 OK (server-side session minting outside Auth.js is fragile). Audit `auth.invite.accepted`. |\n| FR-17 | Password Policy | `src/lib/validators/auth.ts` — Zod schema shared client+server. Common-password list lives in `src/server/auth/common-passwords.txt` (server-only file read) → split: client checks length/letter/digit/≠email; server additionally checks common list. FR-18 checklist can't show the common-list rule live without shipping the list; either ship the list (~8 KB) to client or show that rule only on submit. Design decision. |\n| FR-18 | Password Policy / Auth UI | Shared `PasswordChecklist` component (MUI List + icons) used by invite, reset and change-password pages. |\n| FR-19 | Password Reset Flow / Lockout & Rate Limiting | `POST /api/auth/forgot-password` — identical response body and similar timing whether or not user exists (NFR-3 spirit). Two rate-limit keys: `forgot:email:<email>` (3/h), `forgot:ip:<ip>` (10/h). Over limit: still the generic message, or RATE_LIMITED 429? PRD says \"always responds\" → design must decide; 429 on IP limit is safe, email limit should stay silent to avoid enumeration. |\n| FR-20 | Password Reset Flow | Only ACTIVE users get a token; `invalidateUnused(userId,'PASSWORD_RESET')` then `createToken(…, 60 min)` then `sendAuthEmail`. Unknown email → no DB write. |\n| FR-21 | Password Reset Flow | `POST /api/auth/reset-password` transaction: verify → policy → hash → `passwordChangedAt=now`, `failedLoginCount=0`, `lockedUntil=null` → consume. Audit `auth.password.reset`. Success toast after redirect needs cross-navigation signal (`/login?reset=1` query param read on login page → `useToast`). |\n| FR-22 | Email Stub | `src/server/auth/email.ts` `sendAuthEmail(user, kind, link)` → `logger.info`. Single seam PRD-04 swaps for EmailOutbox. Link base URL from env (`AUTH_URL`/`NEXTAUTH_URL`). |\n| FR-23 | Change Password | `/account/security` page inside `(app)` (authenticated). `POST /api/account/password` → `requireUser()` → bcrypt compare current → policy → hash → `passwordChangedAt=now`. \"Re-issue current session\": after `passwordChangedAt` bump, current JWT's `iat` < `passwordChangedAt` would be invalidated by FR-11 → must refresh current token (client `update()` from `useSession`, with jwt callback trigger `update` resetting `iat`/`checkedAt`). Key subtlety for design. Wrong current password → 400 with field-level `details` for the form. |\n| FR-24 | App Shell & Nav | Add menu item in `shell.tsx` user menu → `/account/security`. All roles. |\n| FR-25 | Users Service | No new `createUser`; confirm P2002 on email → CONFLICT via existing `http.ts` mapping. Keep `setUserStatus(actorId, userId, status)` and `unlockUser(userId)` (resolves blocker 2 per intake defaults). Ensure `createUser` lower-cases email (FR-2). |\n| FR-26 | Users Service / Token Module | `createInviteToken(userId)` in `users.ts` → `tokens.invalidateUnused` + `tokens.createToken(userId,'INVITE',{days:7})`. |\n| FR-27 | Users Service / Email Stub | `sendInvite` body: `createInviteToken` → `sendAuthEmail(user,'invite',link)`; keep return `{id, inviteSentAt}` for existing callers. |\n| FR-28 | Authorization Helpers | `requireRole.ts`: delete `PLACEHOLDER_USER`; add `getCurrentUser()` (wraps `auth()`, memoised per request with React `cache()`), `requireUser()`. Server pages vs route handlers need different failure behaviour (redirect vs throw). Cleanest: separate fns or an option, since there's no reliable runtime way to detect context. Resolves blocker 3: **go async**; ~48 call sites add `await` (all already in async fns). No `permissions.ts`. |\n| FR-29 | Authorization Helpers | `requireRole(...roles)` → `Promise<SessionUser>` (superset of `{id, role}` so `course-access.ts` compiles unchanged). Throw `AppError('FORBIDDEN')` in handlers; pages redirect `/forbidden` (route exists). No `can()`. |\n| FR-30 | Audit | Add optional `request?: Request` to `AuditInput`; `ip = getClientIp(request)` (first value of `x-forwarded-for`, trimmed). Keep inside try/catch. Shared helper with FR-7. |\n| FR-31 | Audit / Security | Enforce by construction: audit callers pass explicit metadata objects; optionally a small denylist strip (`password`, `passwordHash`, `token`) in `audit()` as defence-in-depth. Covered by test. |\n| FR-32 | App Shell & Nav | `shell.tsx` reads session — either the `(app)/layout.tsx` server component passes `getCurrentUser()` as a prop, or `SessionProvider` + `useSession`. Prop-passing avoids a client fetch and flash. Sign out → `signOut({ redirectTo: '/login' })`. Role badge + initials via MUI `Chip`/`Avatar`. |\n| FR-33 | App Shell & Nav | Filter `navigation.ts` entries by role (server-side, before passing to shell). UX only — NFR-7 still applies on every page. |\n| FR-34 | Auth Core / REST API | `src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts` exporting `{ GET, POST } = handlers` from `auth.ts`. |\n| FR-35 | Seed | Extend `prisma/seed.ts` idempotently (`upsert` by email): 6 ACTIVE users (bcrypt cost 12 of `SchoolHub123!`) + `invited@` INVITED with invite created via `createInviteToken` and link logged. Keep `office@`. Seeding goes through `tokens.ts` (FR-13 single-module rule). |\n| FR-36 | Test Harness | Unit (pure fns: policy, token hash/expiry, `computeLockout`, rate limiter, `safeCallbackUrl`) → design should keep these logic pieces as pure exported fns. Integration against DB for authorize/accept/reset/change. Playwright needs seeded users (FR-35) and the reset link from logs → e2e must read the token from DB or a test hook rather than email. |\n| FR-37 | Scope Discipline / Auth Core | No `/signup` route and no sign-up endpoint; the only path to ACTIVE is FR-16 (or admin `setUserStatus`). Middleware public list must not include any sign-up path. |\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| NFR-1 | Security / Token Module | SHA-256 at rest; `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers. Unique index on `AuthToken.tokenHash` makes lookup O(1). |\n| NFR-2 | Security / Auth Core | Auth.js defaults already give HttpOnly, `__Secure-` prefix + Secure in prod, SameSite=Lax, CSRF on its own endpoints. **Custom POST routes (`accept-invite`, `forgot-password`, `reset-password`, `account/password`) are outside Auth.js CSRF** — design must cover them (Origin/`Sec-Fetch-Site` same-origin check in route or middleware; SameSite=Lax alone doesn't protect unauthenticated endpoints). |\n| NFR-3 | Security / Credential Verification | Precompute a dummy bcrypt hash at module load; compare against it when the user is missing so timing matches. Same idea for forgot-password (no branch-visible timing difference). |\n| NFR-4 | Performance | bcrypt cost 12 ≈ 250 ms; budget fine. Keep authorize to 1 user query + 1 update + async audit; don't await non-critical writes serially beyond that. |\n| NFR-5 | Accessibility | Form `Alert` inside `aria-live=\"polite\"` region; on submit-with-errors focus first invalid `TextField` (`inputRef`). Shared form-error pattern across 5 auth forms. |\n| NFR-6 | Design System (MUI-only) | All auth pages, checklist, shell menu changes use MUI v9 only; dev must look up each component via mui-mcp. mui-mcp tools are visible in this environment (`useMuiDocs`, `fetchDocs`) — blocker 4 currently looks unblocked, dev must still confirm. |\n| NFR-7 | Authorization Helpers | Every new route handler and `(app)` page calls `requireUser()`/`requireRole()`; middleware is a first gate, not the only one. |\n| NFR-8 | REST API Surface | All custom auth routes: Zod parse → service → `{data}` / `{error:{code,message,details}}` using existing `http.ts` helpers. Auth.js catch-all is the sole exception (its own protocol). |\n| NFR-9 | Scope Discipline | Reuse `audit.ts`, `users.ts`, `requireRole.ts`, `useToast`, `AppError`; no renames; `course-access.ts` / `student-access.ts` untouched (constrains FR-29 return type). |\n| NFR-10 | Test Harness | Lint/typecheck/test green — the async `requireRole` change (FR-28/29) touches ~42 files, so typecheck is the safety net. `logger` only; `console.log` banned except the dev email transport. |\n| NFR-11 | Scope Discipline | No admin UI, SSO, MFA, magic links, or email infra. |\n| NFR-12 | Data Model / Auth UI | Timestamps UTC in DB. Lockout \"N minutes\" is a duration (timezone-independent); `lastLoginAt` display formatted in `SchoolSettings.timezone` (existing settings model). |\n\n## Cross-cutting items for `arch-design-system`\n\n1. **Edge/Node split** (FR-4, FR-11, FR-12): `auth.config.ts` edge-safe for middleware; `auth.ts` Node with Prisma/bcrypt.\n2. **requireRole goes async** (FR-28/29, blocker 3): ~48 sites, all already in async fns; return type superset of `{id, role}`.\n3. **Session re-issue after password change** (FR-11 vs FR-23): current session must survive its own `passwordChangedAt` bump.\n4. **CSRF for custom auth POSTs** (NFR-2): not covered by Auth.js.\n5. **Shared `getClientIp(request)`** (FR-7, FR-19, FR-30).\n6. **Shared password UI + Zod schema** (FR-15/17/18/21/23); decide whether the common-password list ships to the client.\n7. **Rate limiter is per-process** (FR-7/FR-19): fine for this PRD, Redis if multi-instance.\n8. **Additive migration only** (FR-1, blocker 1): two AuditLog indexes plus any missing User columns.\n\n## Blockers carried forward from intake\n\n- FR-1 schema divergences → resolved by additive migration (above).\n- FR-25 signature mismatch → keep the existing signatures.\n- FR-28/29 sync vs async → recommend async (call sites are fewer than intake reported and are all already async).\n- NFR-6 mui-mcp reachability → tools are registered in this environment; dev must still check before writing UI.\n","numLines":114,"startLine":1,"totalLines":114}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VtHpJyfHxWMg4hyMVhfC1o","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
