{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghRp8y5N3YFw2B2o75eW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written `artifacts/architect/system-design.md` for ticket abcd-1. The file that was already there was a stale design from an older ticket (abcd-05), so I overwrote it.\n\nIt's built on the two earlier architect outputs (the tech-stack decisions and the requirements notes) plus a fresh check of the codebase. It has the Overview with the intake blockers, 15 components, data flows, the API list for the next sub-task, and a traceability table covering all 37 FRs and 12 NFRs.\n\n**The four intake blockers:**\n- **Schema differences (FR-1):** add only the two missing AuditLog indexes. `AuditLog.ip` and the extra `ARCHIVED` status stay as they are, and nothing is renamed.\n- **Existing user functions (FR-25):** keep the current `createUser` and `setUserStatus` signatures and don't add duplicates.\n- **`requireRole()` going async (FR-28/29):** it becomes async. There are 50 call sites, not ~94: 33 API route files just add `await`, and 8 server pages switch to new versions that redirect to the login or forbidden page. The two access modules the PRD says not to touch don't actually call `requireRole`, so they stay unchanged. I added a lint rule (`no-floating-promises`) so a missed `await` gets caught.\n- **mui-mcp reachability (NFR-6):** its tools are available in this environment, so it doesn't block design. Dev still has to confirm it's reachable before writing UI.\n\n**Things I found or decided that weren't in the earlier notes:**\n- Because the app uses a `src/` folder, the middleware has to live at `src/middleware.ts`; a file at the project root would never run.\n- Session revocation after a password change uses a custom sign-in time stored in the token, not the token's built-in issue time. Auth.js refreshes that issue time on every session refresh, which would hide an older password change.\n- Auth.js's CSRF protection doesn't cover the four custom POST routes (accept invite, forgot password, reset password, change password). Middleware will reject those requests when the `Origin` header doesn't match the app.\n- On forgot-password, going over the per-IP limit returns a 429 error, but going over the per-email limit still returns the generic success message. A 429 there would reveal that the email had been tried before.\n- After accepting an invite, the browser signs the user in through the normal sign-in flow rather than the server creating a session by hand.\n\nThe `api-contracts.json` and `handoff-to-dev-planning.json` files in the folder are also left over from the old ticket. The next sub-task (`arch-api-contracts`) is the one that replaces them, so I didn't touch them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15378,"cache_read_input_tokens":58545,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15378},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8ecf6ce-8d48-4a6b-879b-0c956e2c759f","uuid":"91aafb2d-7958-4366-b37d-bf0a281df4b4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:39:13.651Z","request_id":"req_011CghRp8UJy3cJEw1825Akq"}
