{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KeDSsHKrMrLrVPwC9tptAV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email Infrastructure (SCH-004)\n2\t\n3\tTicket: SH-4 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T12:10:00Z · Status: complete\n4\t\n5\t**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports confidence 100%, and `package.json`\n6\tconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3, TypeScript 5.5 strict,\n7\tPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5 (material, icons, x-data-grid) + emotion,\n8\tzod 3.23, date-fns 3.6 / date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1 and Playwright 1.47.\n9\t**This PRD adds no new runtime or dev dependency.** Everything it needs is\n10\talready installed: `nodemailer ^6.9.14` + `@types/nodemailer`,\n11\t`@react-email/components 0.0.25`, `@react-email/render 1.0.1` and `tsx 4.16`.\n12\tThe intake stack fits every FR/NFR, so the ADRs below record only the\n13\timplementation choices the requirements force.\n14\t\n15\t**Brownfield facts checked in the workspace.** These confirm or sharpen intake:\n16\t- `prisma/schema.prisma` declares `EmailStatus`, `Notification` (with\n17\t  `@@index([userId, readAt, createdAt])`), `NotificationPreference` (composite\n18\t  `@@id([userId, type])`) and `EmailOutbox` (with `@@index([status, nextAttemptAt])`, no FK\n19\t  to User, and `updatedAt` documented as the SENDING timestamp). The 4 existing\n20\t  migrations don't create these tables.\n21\t- `@react-email/render@1.0.1`'s `render(el, { plainText?: true })` returns a\n22\t  **`Promise<string>`**. It's async in 1.x, so every render call must be awaited.\n23\t- `src/lib/env.ts` already validates `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` (`smtp|console`, default\n24\t  `console`), `SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, an optional `CRON_SECRET` and `APP_URL`.\n25\t- `src/server/http.ts` already provides `withHandler`, `ok`, `paginated`,\n26\t  `parsePagination`, `parseBody`, `parseQuery` and `AppError`. `src/lib/logger.ts`\n27\t  provides `logger`. `src/lib/dates.ts` provides `formatInSchoolTz`. `src/server/db.ts`\n28\t  exports the `prisma` singleton.\n29\t- The existing `src/components/DataTable` wraps MUI X DataGrid with\n30\t  URL-driven server pagination. `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts` exists.\n31\t- Vitest `include` is `tests/unit/**` only, with `environment: 'jsdom'`.\n32\t  There's no integration test folder yet.\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## ADR-1: One additive Prisma migration for the existing PRD-04 models\n37\t\n38\t**Context.** FR-1 requires the tables. The models are already in\n39\t`schema.prisma` but no migration creates them (an intake blocker). NFR-13\n40\tsays never rename and never re-scaffold. NFR-5 relies on `EmailOutbox.updatedAt`.\n41\t\n42\t**Decision.** Run `prisma migrate dev --name notifications_email` without\n43\ttouching the schema. It generates the `EmailStatus` enum, the 3 tables, their\n44\tindexes and the `Notification`/`NotificationPreference` → `User` cascade FKs.\n45\tKeep the additive `updatedAt` columns exactly as they are declared.\n46\t\n47\t**Consequences.** This is one purely additive migration with nothing destructive. Prisma's\n48\t`@updatedAt` sets the column on every update made through the Prisma client, **but not\n49\ton `$executeRaw`/`$queryRaw` writes**. Any raw SQL that sets `status = 'SENDING'`\n50\tmust also set `\"updatedAt\" = now()` itself (see ADR-3).\n51\t\n52\t_Cites: FR-1, NFR-5, NFR-13_\n53\t\n54\t## ADR-2: Registry as a `const` object with `satisfies`; `keyof typeof` drives notify()\n55\t\n56\t**Context.** FR-2 through FR-5 need a typed registry that rejects unknown\n57\tkeys at compile time and that later PRDs can extend.\n58\t\n59\t**Decision.** In `src/server/notifications/types.ts`, define\n60\t`export const NOTIFICATION_TYPES = { ... } as const satisfies Record<string, NotificationTypeDef>`\n61\tand `export type NotificationType = keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`. Express\n62\tcategories (FR-3) as a string-literal union, `NotificationCategory`, plus a\n63\t`NOTIFICATION_CATEGORIES` tuple that the preferences page and its zod enum use.\n64\t`NotificationTypeDef` adds `emailOnly?: true` / `hidden?: true` for the\n65\t`account.*` types (FR-4, FR-28). Later PRDs extend the registry by adding\n66\tkeys to this one object. Don't build a runtime registration API (YAGNI).\n67\t\n68\t**Consequences.** `NotificationPreference.type` and `Notification.type` stay\n69\tplain `String` columns, because the DB doesn't need an enum for a list that\n70\tgrows with every PRD. The zod validator for the preferences PUT (FR-38,\n71\tFR-41) builds `z.enum(Object.keys(NOTIFICATION_TYPES) as [NotificationType, ...])`.\n72\tIt can't import server-only modules, so `types.ts` must stay free of server\n73\timports (no `prisma`, no `env`) to remain importable from\n74\t`src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\n75\t\n76\t_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-28, FR-38, FR-41_\n77\t\n78\t## ADR-3: Dispatcher claim through one raw-SQL `UPDATE … WHERE id IN (SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED) RETURNING *`\n79\t\n80\t**Context.** FR-17 and NFR-4 require that concurrent dispatchers never send a row\n81\ttwice. NFR-5 requires recovering rows stuck in SENDING for more than 10 minutes. Prisma 5.20\n82\thas no `SKIP LOCKED` in its query API.\n83\t\n84\t**Decision.** Each `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` run:\n85\t1. **Recover.** Run `prisma.emailOutbox.updateMany({ where: { status: 'SENDING', updatedAt: { lt: now - 10min } }, data: { status: 'PENDING' } })`.\n86\t2. **Claim.** Use a single statement through `prisma.$queryRaw`:\n87\t   ```sql\n88\t   UPDATE \"EmailOutbox\" SET status = 'SENDING', \"updatedAt\" = now()\n89\t   WHERE id IN (\n90\t     SELECT id FROM \"EmailOutbox\"\n91\t     WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND \"nextAttemptAt\" <= now()\n92\t     ORDER BY \"nextAttemptAt\" LIMIT ${batchSize}\n93\t     FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)\n94\t   RETURNING *;\n95\t   ```\n96\t   It runs as one autocommit statement, so the row lock is held only for the flip and is released\n97\t   before any SMTP call. This avoids long transactions, and once a row is\n98\t   SENDING no other dispatcher's `WHERE status='PENDING'` can match it.\n99\t3. **Send each row**, then mark it SENT (`sentAt`) or reschedule it through Prisma `update` (ADR-4).\n100\t\n101\t**Consequences.** This is the only raw SQL in the PRD. Use tagged-template `$queryRaw`, never\n102\t`$queryRawUnsafe`. Cast the enum literal (`'SENDING'::\"EmailStatus\"`) because\n103\tPostgres won't implicitly cast text to an enum in a `SET`. A crash mid-send leaves the row SENDING,\n104\tand step 1 recovers it after 10 minutes. That gives at-least-once delivery on crash, which\n105\tthe PRD accepts. The two-concurrent-callers and stuck-row behavior is covered\n106\tby NFR-15 integration tests (ADR-9).\n107\t\n108\t_Cites: FR-17, NFR-4, NFR-5, NFR-15, AC-4_\n109\t\n110\t## ADR-4: Retry backoff as a pure lookup table\n111\t\n112\t**Context.** FR-18 sets backoff delays of 1/5/15/60/240 min for attempts 1–5\n113\tand FAILED after the 5th failure. NFR-14 requires a unit test for it.\n114\t\n115\t**Decision.** `src/server/email/backoff.ts` exports `const BACKOFF_MIN = [1, 5, 15, 60, 240]`\n116\tand a pure `nextRetry(attempts, now): { status: 'PENDING', nextAttemptAt } | { status: 'FAILED' }`,\n117\twhere `attempts` is the count after incrementing. No retry library.\n118\t\n119\t**Consequences.** This is a trivially unit-testable pure function. The admin\n120\t\"Retry now\" action (FR-32) just resets `attempts=0, status=PENDING, nextAttemptAt=now()`.\n121\t\n122\t_Cites: FR-18, FR-32, NFR-14, AC-3_\n123\t\n124\t## ADR-5: Email transport: Nodemailer for SMTP, a logger-free console transport, a lazy singleton\n125\t\n126\t**Context.** FR-21 selects the transport by `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`. NFR-9 requires\n127\tHTML + text parts and a `List-Unsubscribe` header. NFR-11 bans `console.log`\n128\texcept in the console transport.\n129\t\n130\t**Decision.** `src/server/email/transport.ts` exports `sendMail({ to, toName, subject, html, text })`.\n131\t- `smtp`: `nodemailer.createTransport({ host, port, secure, auth })`, created\n132\t  lazily once per process. It sends `{ from: EMAIL_FROM, html, text, headers: { 'List-Unsubscribe': '<${APP_URL}/account/notifications>' } }`.\n133\t  Nodemailer builds `multipart/alternative` automatically when both `html` and `text` are set.\n134\t  It throws a clear error at first send if `SMTP_HOST` or `EMAIL_FROM` is missing.\n135\t- `console`: `console.log` of the subject, recipient and text body (the one sanctioned use, NFR-11).\n136\t\n137\tUse a plain `if` on the env value, with no transport interface or factory, because\n138\tthere are only two branches.\n139\t\n140\t**Consequences.** Dev and CI never need SMTP credentials. Tests can assert outbox rows\n141\tand dispatcher state transitions without a mail server. The SMTP failure path\n142\t(AC-3) is tested by stubbing `sendMail` with `vi.mock`.\n143\t\n144\t_Cites: FR-17, FR-21, NFR-9, NFR-11_\n145\t\n146\t## ADR-6: Email templates with @react-email/components, rendered at enqueue time\n147\t\n148\t**Context.** FR-13 through FR-15 and FR-16 require HTML and text emails. NFR-7 requires escaping user\n149\ttext. NFR-3 says no request may wait on SMTP.\n150\t\n151\t**Decision.** Templates are `.tsx` components in `src/server/email/templates/`\n152\t(`BaseLayout`, `Invite`, `PasswordReset`, `GenericNotification`).\n153\t`src/server/email/render.ts` exposes\n154\t`renderEmail(template, data) → Promise<{ html, text }>`, which calls\n155\t`await render(el)` and `await render(el, { plainText: true })` from\n156\t`@react-email/render` 1.0.1. Rendering happens **inside the request** when\n157\t`notify()`/`sendAuthEmail()` enqueue. The stored `html`/`text` columns\n158\tare then a point-in-time snapshot, and the dispatcher only sends. `BaseLayout` reads\n159\t`SchoolSettings` once per `notify()` call, not once per recipient.\n160\t\n161\t**Consequences.** Escaping (NFR-7) comes from React's JSX text escaping. Never use\n162\t`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in templates. Rendering is CPU work. For 1,000 recipients\n163\t(NFR-1), render the `generic-notification` template **once per notify() call**,\n164\tnot per recipient. This works only if the template data has no per-recipient fields,\n165\tso keep greetings generic in generic-notification. If a later PRD needs per-recipient\n166\tpersonalisation, revisit NFR-1. Email templates are exempt from the MUI rule (NFR-10).\n167\t\n168\t_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-16, NFR-1, NFR-3, NFR-7, NFR-10_\n169\t\n170\t## ADR-7: Background dispatch as a cron-hit HTTP route plus an optional tsx worker loop; no queue library\n171\t\n172\t**Context.** FR-19 and FR-20 require both a cron endpoint and a worker script. NFR-3 requires dispatch within 60 s.\n173\tNFR-17 rules out new infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ are implicitly out).\n174\t\n175\t**Decision.**\n176\t- `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` compares `Authorization: Bearer <token>`\n177\t  against `env.CRON_SECRET` with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length\n178\t  buffers. If `CRON_SECRET` is unset, it always returns 401 (NFR-8). The route\n179\t  calls `dispatchEmails()` and returns `{ sent, failed, retried }`, then\n180\t  calls `purgeOldNotifications()` if more than 24 h have passed since the\n181\t  module-level `lastPurgeAt` (FR-27).\n182\t- `scripts/worker-email.ts` runs a `while (true) { await dispatchEmails(); await sleep(15_000) }`\n183\t  loop with `try/catch` + `logger.error` around each tick. It calls the service\n184\t  directly, not over HTTP, and catches SIGINT/SIGTERM to exit cleanly. It imports\n185\t  through `tsx`, which resolves the `@/` tsconfig paths.\n186\t\n187\t**Consequences.** A 15 s worker tick, or a cron schedule of 1 minute or less, meets NFR-3.\n188\tThe `lastPurgeAt` value is tracked per process, so it resets on restart and\n189\tpurges run on every instance. The PRD accepts this. Mark it\n190\t`ponytail: in-memory last-run, move to a DB row if purge cost matters`.\n191\t\n192\t_Cites: FR-19, FR-20, FR-27, NFR-3, NFR-8, NFR-17, AC-6_\n193\t\n194\t## ADR-8: Client UI with MUI v9 only, plain `fetch` polling, and no data-fetching library\n195\t\n196\t**Context.** FR-22 through FR-26, FR-28 through FR-31 and NFR-10 cover the UI. FR-23 requires a 30 s poll that pauses\n197\twhen the tab is hidden, and the PRD excludes websockets (NFR-17).\n198\t\n199\t**Decision.**\n200\t- **Bell (FR-22–25).** A client component using `IconButton` + `Badge`\n201\t  (`max={9}` renders \"9+\" natively) + `Popover` + `List`/`ListItemButton`.\n202\t  Polling is a `setInterval(30_000)` started and stopped from a\n203\t  `visibilitychange` listener in one `useEffect`. Don't add SWR or React Query,\n204\t  because they aren't installed and one effect covers the need. Use `Typography` with\n205\t  `sx={{ display: '-webkit-box', WebkitLineClamp: 2, WebkitBoxOrient: 'vertical', overflow: 'hidden' }}`\n206\t  for the 2-line clamp.\n207\t- **Relative time (FR-24).** Use `formatDistanceToNowStrict` from the installed\n208\t  `date-fns`. Use `formatInSchoolTz` for absolute times (NFR-12).\n209\t- **/notifications (FR-26).** Use MUI `Tabs` (All/Unread), a `Select` for the category, a `List`, and\n210\t  `Pagination`.\n211\t- **/account/notifications (FR-28–30).** Use `Table` with a full-width category\n212\t  `TableRow` header per group (intake's default), and `Switch` + `Tooltip`\n213\t  \"Required by the school\" on a disabled switch. Wrap the disabled switch in a `<span>` so the\n214\t  tooltip still fires. Use optimistic local state with rollback, plus the existing `useToast`.\n215\t- **/admin/email-log (FR-31–32).** Reuse the existing `src/components/DataTable`\n216\t  (MUI X DataGrid, URL-driven pagination), with a status `Select` and a row-action `Button`.\n217\t\n218\tThe mui-mcp server is reachable from this session (`useMuiDocs`,\n219\t`fetchDocs` and `generateReactCode` tools are listed), so the NFR-10 blocker doesn't\n220\tstand at architect time. Dev must still call `useMuiDocs` → `fetchDocs`\n221\tper component before writing UI, and QA greps for banned imports.\n222\t\n223\t**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. The bell keeps its local state, and\n224\t\"mark read\" refetches the count instead of introducing a shared store.\n225\t\n226\t_Cites: FR-22, FR-23, FR-24, FR-25, FR-26, FR-28, FR-29, FR-30, FR-31, FR-32, NFR-10, NFR-12, NFR-17_\n227\t\n228\t## ADR-9: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; integration tests gated on a test DB\n229\t\n230\t**Context.** NFR-14 lists unit tests, NFR-15 integration tests against a DB,\n231\tand NFR-16 Playwright tests. Vitest currently only includes `tests/unit/**`\n232\t(jsdom), and NFR-13 requires `npm run test` to keep passing on machines without a DB.\n233\t\n234\t**Decision.**\n235\t- Unit tests go in `tests/unit/`: preference resolution, `nextRetry`, link validation\n236\t  and `notifyStudentAndParents` dedupe. These are pure functions, with Prisma mocked by `vi.mock` where needed.\n237\t- Integration tests go in `tests/integration/*.test.ts`. Add that glob to `vitest.config.ts`\n238\t  `include`, set `// @vitest-environment node` per file, and wrap each suite\n239\t  in `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)` so a plain `npm run test`\n240\t  skips it instead of failing. Write the concurrency test as\n241\t  `Promise.all([dispatchEmails(), dispatchEmails()])` over 10 seeded PENDING rows,\n242\t  with `sendMail` mocked to count calls, and assert each id is sent exactly once.\n243\t- Playwright tests go in `tests/e2e/notifications.spec.ts`, covering the bell count after the admin\n244\t  test button, mark-all-read and a persisted preference toggle.\n245\t\n246\tNo new test tooling.\n247\t\n248\t**Consequences.** CI must set `TEST_DATABASE_URL` for NFR-15 to actually run.\n249\tDev-planning should note this, because a green `npm run test` without it proves nothing about locking.\n250\t\n251\t_Cites: NFR-13, NFR-14, NFR-15, NFR-16, AC-4_\n252\t\n253\t## ADR-10: Link validation and field limits as shared pure helpers; validate in notify() without throwing\n254\t\n255\t**Context.** NFR-6 covers open-redirect-safe links, NFR-18 the title/body limits and\n256\tFR-11 says notify() never throws.\n257\t\n258\t**Decision.** Add `isSafeRelativeLink(s)` to `src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\n259\tIt returns true only when the string starts with `/`, the second character is not `/` or `\\`, the\n260\tstring contains no `\\`, and it has no control characters. It's shared by notify(), the zod schemas and\n261\tthe bell's `router.push`. In notify(), an unsafe link is dropped (stored as `null`)\n262\twith `logger.warn`, and title/body are truncated to 140/500 characters with `logger.warn`\n263\t(the intake default). The whole notify() body is wrapped in `try/catch → logger.error`.\n264\t\n265\t**Consequences.** A bad link degrades to a notification that can't be clicked, never\n266\tan exception in the caller's flow. A single regex-free function is trivially unit-tested (NFR-14).\n267\t\n268\t_Cites: FR-6, FR-11, NFR-6, NFR-14, NFR-18_\n269\t\n270\t---\n271\t\n272\t### Notes for downstream sub-tasks\n273\t- **Intake blocker FR-4/FR-16 (confirmed).** `sendAuthEmail()` renders the\n274\t  invite/password-reset template and inserts one `EmailOutbox` row directly,\n275\t  with no Notification row and no preference lookup. The `account.*` types are\n276\t  `emailOnly` + `hidden`. The signature and both callers (users.ts:396, auth.ts:54) stay unchanged.\n277\t- **NFR-1/FR-10 budget.** notify() makes 1 query each for users, preferences and\n278\t  parent links where relevant, then 1 `createMany` for notifications and 1 for outbox\n279\t  rows. That's within the limit of 3 round-trips each. Render once (ADR-6).\n280\t- **No new dependencies anywhere in this PRD.**\n281\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a6de621-bc72-44a5-9725-5c1b7f039997","uuid":"79b8496e-41ce-4e7b-96d3-1d65cb44dd39","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:01:35.413Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-04 Notifications & Email Infrastructure (SCH-004)\n\nTicket: SH-4 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-04T12:10:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports confidence 100%, and `package.json`\nconfirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3, TypeScript 5.5 strict,\nPrisma 5.20 / PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5 (material, icons, x-data-grid) + emotion,\nzod 3.23, date-fns 3.6 / date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1 and Playwright 1.47.\n**This PRD adds no new runtime or dev dependency.** Everything it needs is\nalready installed: `nodemailer ^6.9.14` + `@types/nodemailer`,\n`@react-email/components 0.0.25`, `@react-email/render 1.0.1` and `tsx 4.16`.\nThe intake stack fits every FR/NFR, so the ADRs below record only the\nimplementation choices the requirements force.\n\n**Brownfield facts checked in the workspace.** These confirm or sharpen intake:\n- `prisma/schema.prisma` declares `EmailStatus`, `Notification` (with\n  `@@index([userId, readAt, createdAt])`), `NotificationPreference` (composite\n  `@@id([userId, type])`) and `EmailOutbox` (with `@@index([status, nextAttemptAt])`, no FK\n  to User, and `updatedAt` documented as the SENDING timestamp). The 4 existing\n  migrations don't create these tables.\n- `@react-email/render@1.0.1`'s `render(el, { plainText?: true })` returns a\n  **`Promise<string>`**. It's async in 1.x, so every render call must be awaited.\n- `src/lib/env.ts` already validates `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` (`smtp|console`, default\n  `console`), `SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, an optional `CRON_SECRET` and `APP_URL`.\n- `src/server/http.ts` already provides `withHandler`, `ok`, `paginated`,\n  `parsePagination`, `parseBody`, `parseQuery` and `AppError`. `src/lib/logger.ts`\n  provides `logger`. `src/lib/dates.ts` provides `formatInSchoolTz`. `src/server/db.ts`\n  exports the `prisma` singleton.\n- The existing `src/components/DataTable` wraps MUI X DataGrid with\n  URL-driven server pagination. `src/components/Toast/useToast.ts` exists.\n- Vitest `include` is `tests/unit/**` only, with `environment: 'jsdom'`.\n  There's no integration test folder yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: One additive Prisma migration for the existing PRD-04 models\n\n**Context.** FR-1 requires the tables. The models are already in\n`schema.prisma` but no migration creates them (an intake blocker). NFR-13\nsays never rename and never re-scaffold. NFR-5 relies on `EmailOutbox.updatedAt`.\n\n**Decision.** Run `prisma migrate dev --name notifications_email` without\ntouching the schema. It generates the `EmailStatus` enum, the 3 tables, their\nindexes and the `Notification`/`NotificationPreference` → `User` cascade FKs.\nKeep the additive `updatedAt` columns exactly as they are declared.\n\n**Consequences.** This is one purely additive migration with nothing destructive. Prisma's\n`@updatedAt` sets the column on every update made through the Prisma client, **but not\non `$executeRaw`/`$queryRaw` writes**. Any raw SQL that sets `status = 'SENDING'`\nmust also set `\"updatedAt\" = now()` itself (see ADR-3).\n\n_Cites: FR-1, NFR-5, NFR-13_\n\n## ADR-2: Registry as a `const` object with `satisfies`; `keyof typeof` drives notify()\n\n**Context.** FR-2 through FR-5 need a typed registry that rejects unknown\nkeys at compile time and that later PRDs can extend.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/notifications/types.ts`, define\n`export const NOTIFICATION_TYPES = { ... } as const satisfies Record<string, NotificationTypeDef>`\nand `export type NotificationType = keyof typeof NOTIFICATION_TYPES`. Express\ncategories (FR-3) as a string-literal union, `NotificationCategory`, plus a\n`NOTIFICATION_CATEGORIES` tuple that the preferences page and its zod enum use.\n`NotificationTypeDef` adds `emailOnly?: true` / `hidden?: true` for the\n`account.*` types (FR-4, FR-28). Later PRDs extend the registry by adding\nkeys to this one object. Don't build a runtime registration API (YAGNI).\n\n**Consequences.** `NotificationPreference.type` and `Notification.type` stay\nplain `String` columns, because the DB doesn't need an enum for a list that\ngrows with every PRD. The zod validator for the preferences PUT (FR-38,\nFR-41) builds `z.enum(Object.keys(NOTIFICATION_TYPES) as [NotificationType, ...])`.\nIt can't import server-only modules, so `types.ts` must stay free of server\nimports (no `prisma`, no `env`) to remain importable from\n`src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\n\n_Cites: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-5, FR-28, FR-38, FR-41_\n\n## ADR-3: Dispatcher claim through one raw-SQL `UPDATE … WHERE id IN (SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED) RETURNING *`\n\n**Context.** FR-17 and NFR-4 require that concurrent dispatchers never send a row\ntwice. NFR-5 requires recovering rows stuck in SENDING for more than 10 minutes. Prisma 5.20\nhas no `SKIP LOCKED` in its query API.\n\n**Decision.** Each `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` run:\n1. **Recover.** Run `prisma.emailOutbox.updateMany({ where: { status: 'SENDING', updatedAt: { lt: now - 10min } }, data: { status: 'PENDING' } })`.\n2. **Claim.** Use a single statement through `prisma.$queryRaw`:\n   ```sql\n   UPDATE \"EmailOutbox\" SET status = 'SENDING', \"updatedAt\" = now()\n   WHERE id IN (\n     SELECT id FROM \"EmailOutbox\"\n     WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND \"nextAttemptAt\" <= now()\n     ORDER BY \"nextAttemptAt\" LIMIT ${batchSize}\n     FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)\n   RETURNING *;\n   ```\n   It runs as one autocommit statement, so the row lock is held only for the flip and is released\n   before any SMTP call. This avoids long transactions, and once a row is\n   SENDING no other dispatcher's `WHERE status='PENDING'` can match it.\n3. **Send each row**, then mark it SENT (`sentAt`) or reschedule it through Prisma `update` (ADR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** This is the only raw SQL in the PRD. Use tagged-template `$queryRaw`, never\n`$queryRawUnsafe`. Cast the enum literal (`'SENDING'::\"EmailStatus\"`) because\nPostgres won't implicitly cast text to an enum in a `SET`. A crash mid-send leaves the row SENDING,\nand step 1 recovers it after 10 minutes. That gives at-least-once delivery on crash, which\nthe PRD accepts. The two-concurrent-callers and stuck-row behavior is covered\nby NFR-15 integration tests (ADR-9).\n\n_Cites: FR-17, NFR-4, NFR-5, NFR-15, AC-4_\n\n## ADR-4: Retry backoff as a pure lookup table\n\n**Context.** FR-18 sets backoff delays of 1/5/15/60/240 min for attempts 1–5\nand FAILED after the 5th failure. NFR-14 requires a unit test for it.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/email/backoff.ts` exports `const BACKOFF_MIN = [1, 5, 15, 60, 240]`\nand a pure `nextRetry(attempts, now): { status: 'PENDING', nextAttemptAt } | { status: 'FAILED' }`,\nwhere `attempts` is the count after incrementing. No retry library.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a trivially unit-testable pure function. The admin\n\"Retry now\" action (FR-32) just resets `attempts=0, status=PENDING, nextAttemptAt=now()`.\n\n_Cites: FR-18, FR-32, NFR-14, AC-3_\n\n## ADR-5: Email transport: Nodemailer for SMTP, a logger-free console transport, a lazy singleton\n\n**Context.** FR-21 selects the transport by `EMAIL_TRANSPORT`. NFR-9 requires\nHTML + text parts and a `List-Unsubscribe` header. NFR-11 bans `console.log`\nexcept in the console transport.\n\n**Decision.** `src/server/email/transport.ts` exports `sendMail({ to, toName, subject, html, text })`.\n- `smtp`: `nodemailer.createTransport({ host, port, secure, auth })`, created\n  lazily once per process. It sends `{ from: EMAIL_FROM, html, text, headers: { 'List-Unsubscribe': '<${APP_URL}/account/notifications>' } }`.\n  Nodemailer builds `multipart/alternative` automatically when both `html` and `text` are set.\n  It throws a clear error at first send if `SMTP_HOST` or `EMAIL_FROM` is missing.\n- `console`: `console.log` of the subject, recipient and text body (the one sanctioned use, NFR-11).\n\nUse a plain `if` on the env value, with no transport interface or factory, because\nthere are only two branches.\n\n**Consequences.** Dev and CI never need SMTP credentials. Tests can assert outbox rows\nand dispatcher state transitions without a mail server. The SMTP failure path\n(AC-3) is tested by stubbing `sendMail` with `vi.mock`.\n\n_Cites: FR-17, FR-21, NFR-9, NFR-11_\n\n## ADR-6: Email templates with @react-email/components, rendered at enqueue time\n\n**Context.** FR-13 through FR-15 and FR-16 require HTML and text emails. NFR-7 requires escaping user\ntext. NFR-3 says no request may wait on SMTP.\n\n**Decision.** Templates are `.tsx` components in `src/server/email/templates/`\n(`BaseLayout`, `Invite`, `PasswordReset`, `GenericNotification`).\n`src/server/email/render.ts` exposes\n`renderEmail(template, data) → Promise<{ html, text }>`, which calls\n`await render(el)` and `await render(el, { plainText: true })` from\n`@react-email/render` 1.0.1. Rendering happens **inside the request** when\n`notify()`/`sendAuthEmail()` enqueue. The stored `html`/`text` columns\nare then a point-in-time snapshot, and the dispatcher only sends. `BaseLayout` reads\n`SchoolSettings` once per `notify()` call, not once per recipient.\n\n**Consequences.** Escaping (NFR-7) comes from React's JSX text escaping. Never use\n`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in templates. Rendering is CPU work. For 1,000 recipients\n(NFR-1), render the `generic-notification` template **once per notify() call**,\nnot per recipient. This works only if the template data has no per-recipient fields,\nso keep greetings generic in generic-notification. If a later PRD needs per-recipient\npersonalisation, revisit NFR-1. Email templates are exempt from the MUI rule (NFR-10).\n\n_Cites: FR-13, FR-14, FR-15, FR-16, NFR-1, NFR-3, NFR-7, NFR-10_\n\n## ADR-7: Background dispatch as a cron-hit HTTP route plus an optional tsx worker loop; no queue library\n\n**Context.** FR-19 and FR-20 require both a cron endpoint and a worker script. NFR-3 requires dispatch within 60 s.\nNFR-17 rules out new infrastructure (Redis/BullMQ are implicitly out).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` compares `Authorization: Bearer <token>`\n  against `env.CRON_SECRET` with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length\n  buffers. If `CRON_SECRET` is unset, it always returns 401 (NFR-8). The route\n  calls `dispatchEmails()` and returns `{ sent, failed, retried }`, then\n  calls `purgeOldNotifications()` if more than 24 h have passed since the\n  module-level `lastPurgeAt` (FR-27).\n- `scripts/worker-email.ts` runs a `while (true) { await dispatchEmails(); await sleep(15_000) }`\n  loop with `try/catch` + `logger.error` around each tick. It calls the service\n  directly, not over HTTP, and catches SIGINT/SIGTERM to exit cleanly. It imports\n  through `tsx`, which resolves the `@/` tsconfig paths.\n\n**Consequences.** A 15 s worker tick, or a cron schedule of 1 minute or less, meets NFR-3.\nThe `lastPurgeAt` value is tracked per process, so it resets on restart and\npurges run on every instance. The PRD accepts this. Mark it\n`ponytail: in-memory last-run, move to a DB row if purge cost matters`.\n\n_Cites: FR-19, FR-20, FR-27, NFR-3, NFR-8, NFR-17, AC-6_\n\n## ADR-8: Client UI with MUI v9 only, plain `fetch` polling, and no data-fetching library\n\n**Context.** FR-22 through FR-26, FR-28 through FR-31 and NFR-10 cover the UI. FR-23 requires a 30 s poll that pauses\nwhen the tab is hidden, and the PRD excludes websockets (NFR-17).\n\n**Decision.**\n- **Bell (FR-22–25).** A client component using `IconButton` + `Badge`\n  (`max={9}` renders \"9+\" natively) + `Popover` + `List`/`ListItemButton`.\n  Polling is a `setInterval(30_000)` started and stopped from a\n  `visibilitychange` listener in one `useEffect`. Don't add SWR or React Query,\n  because they aren't installed and one effect covers the need. Use `Typography` with\n  `sx={{ display: '-webkit-box', WebkitLineClamp: 2, WebkitBoxOrient: 'vertical', overflow: 'hidden' }}`\n  for the 2-line clamp.\n- **Relative time (FR-24).** Use `formatDistanceToNowStrict` from the installed\n  `date-fns`. Use `formatInSchoolTz` for absolute times (NFR-12).\n- **/notifications (FR-26).** Use MUI `Tabs` (All/Unread), a `Select` for the category, a `List`, and\n  `Pagination`.\n- **/account/notifications (FR-28–30).** Use `Table` with a full-width category\n  `TableRow` header per group (intake's default), and `Switch` + `Tooltip`\n  \"Required by the school\" on a disabled switch. Wrap the disabled switch in a `<span>` so the\n  tooltip still fires. Use optimistic local state with rollback, plus the existing `useToast`.\n- **/admin/email-log (FR-31–32).** Reuse the existing `src/components/DataTable`\n  (MUI X DataGrid, URL-driven pagination), with a status `Select` and a row-action `Button`.\n\nThe mui-mcp server is reachable from this session (`useMuiDocs`,\n`fetchDocs` and `generateReactCode` tools are listed), so the NFR-10 blocker doesn't\nstand at architect time. Dev must still call `useMuiDocs` → `fetchDocs`\nper component before writing UI, and QA greps for banned imports.\n\n**Consequences.** No new UI dependency. The bell keeps its local state, and\n\"mark read\" refetches the count instead of introducing a shared store.\n\n_Cites: FR-22, FR-23, FR-24, FR-25, FR-26, FR-28, FR-29, FR-30, FR-31, FR-32, NFR-10, NFR-12, NFR-17_\n\n## ADR-9: Tests with the existing Vitest + Playwright; integration tests gated on a test DB\n\n**Context.** NFR-14 lists unit tests, NFR-15 integration tests against a DB,\nand NFR-16 Playwright tests. Vitest currently only includes `tests/unit/**`\n(jsdom), and NFR-13 requires `npm run test` to keep passing on machines without a DB.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Unit tests go in `tests/unit/`: preference resolution, `nextRetry`, link validation\n  and `notifyStudentAndParents` dedupe. These are pure functions, with Prisma mocked by `vi.mock` where needed.\n- Integration tests go in `tests/integration/*.test.ts`. Add that glob to `vitest.config.ts`\n  `include`, set `// @vitest-environment node` per file, and wrap each suite\n  in `describe.runIf(process.env.TEST_DATABASE_URL)` so a plain `npm run test`\n  skips it instead of failing. Write the concurrency test as\n  `Promise.all([dispatchEmails(), dispatchEmails()])` over 10 seeded PENDING rows,\n  with `sendMail` mocked to count calls, and assert each id is sent exactly once.\n- Playwright tests go in `tests/e2e/notifications.spec.ts`, covering the bell count after the admin\n  test button, mark-all-read and a persisted preference toggle.\n\nNo new test tooling.\n\n**Consequences.** CI must set `TEST_DATABASE_URL` for NFR-15 to actually run.\nDev-planning should note this, because a green `npm run test` without it proves nothing about locking.\n\n_Cites: NFR-13, NFR-14, NFR-15, NFR-16, AC-4_\n\n## ADR-10: Link validation and field limits as shared pure helpers; validate in notify() without throwing\n\n**Context.** NFR-6 covers open-redirect-safe links, NFR-18 the title/body limits and\nFR-11 says notify() never throws.\n\n**Decision.** Add `isSafeRelativeLink(s)` to `src/lib/validators/notifications.ts`.\nIt returns true only when the string starts with `/`, the second character is not `/` or `\\`, the\nstring contains no `\\`, and it has no control characters. It's shared by notify(), the zod schemas and\nthe bell's `router.push`. In notify(), an unsafe link is dropped (stored as `null`)\nwith `logger.warn`, and title/body are truncated to 140/500 characters with `logger.warn`\n(the intake default). The whole notify() body is wrapped in `try/catch → logger.error`.\n\n**Consequences.** A bad link degrades to a notification that can't be clicked, never\nan exception in the caller's flow. A single regex-free function is trivially unit-tested (NFR-14).\n\n_Cites: FR-6, FR-11, NFR-6, NFR-14, NFR-18_\n\n---\n\n### Notes for downstream sub-tasks\n- **Intake blocker FR-4/FR-16 (confirmed).** `sendAuthEmail()` renders the\n  invite/password-reset template and inserts one `EmailOutbox` row directly,\n  with no Notification row and no preference lookup. The `account.*` types are\n  `emailOnly` + `hidden`. The signature and both callers (users.ts:396, auth.ts:54) stay unchanged.\n- **NFR-1/FR-10 budget.** notify() makes 1 query each for users, preferences and\n  parent links where relevant, then 1 `createMany` for notifications and 1 for outbox\n  rows. That's within the limit of 3 round-trips each. Render once (ADR-6).\n- **No new dependencies anywhere in this PRD.**\n","numLines":281,"startLine":1,"totalLines":281}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KeDSsHKrMrLrVPwC9tptAV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
