{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YBpLLfQTfXf3Czc38c5xPm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)\n2\t\n3\tTicket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete\n4\t\n5\t**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and\n6\t`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,\n7\tPrisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,\n8\tzod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.\n9\tThe stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**\n10\tThe ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force.\n11\t\n12\t**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n13\t\n14\t- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.\n15\t- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.\n16\t- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.\n17\t- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.\n18\t- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.\n19\t- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).\n20\t- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else\n25\t\n26\t**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one\n27\tnon-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could\n28\tjustify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons.\n29\t\n30\t**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin\n31\tconvention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,\n32\t`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)\n33\tuses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons.\n34\t\n35\t**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic\n36\tserver-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,\n37\t`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is.\n38\t\n39\t## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper\n40\t\n41\t**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.\n42\tFR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and\n43\ttoday's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones.\n44\t\n45\t**Decision.**\n46\t- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.\n47\t- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).\n48\t- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.\n49\t- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only.\n50\t\n51\t**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests\n52\tpass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into\n53\tpure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,\n54\tand the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them.\n55\t\n56\t## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`\n57\t\n58\t**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE\n59\ttransaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.\n60\tNFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409.\n61\t\n62\t**Decision.**\n63\t- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.\n64\t- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).\n65\t- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).\n66\t- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.\n67\t- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`.\n68\t\n69\t**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global\n70\t`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter\n71\tthat defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.\n72\tThe NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,\n73\tthen `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled.\n74\t\n75\t## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)\n76\t\n77\t**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day\n78\trule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every\n79\t(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under\n80\t400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this.\n81\t\n82\t**Decision.**\n83\t- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.\n84\t- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.\n85\t- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.\n86\t- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.\n87\t\n88\t**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).\n89\tCeiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows\n90\tfor a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.\n91\t`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`\n92\t\n93\t## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path\n94\t\n95\t**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps\n96\tback. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.\n97\tFR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.\n98\t\n99\t**Decision.**\n100\t- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).\n101\t- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.\n102\t- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).\n103\t- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.\n104\t- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.\n105\t\n106\t**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable\n107\tfor an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still\n108\tbe looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this\n109\tenvironment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.\n110\t\n111\t## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library\n112\t\n113\t**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity\n114\tname and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,\n115\tFirefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.\n116\t\n117\t**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with\n118\tthe grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule\n119\thides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only\n120\theading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background\n121\tcolours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,\n122\tso colour is not required (NFR-4).\n123\t\n124\t**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,\n125\twhich is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright\n126\tcheck uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.\n127\t\n128\t## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup\n129\t\n130\t**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).\n131\t\n132\t**Decision.**\n133\t- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).\n134\t- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).\n135\t- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.\n136\t\n137\t**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t\n141\t## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)\n142\t\n143\t- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.\n144\t- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.\n145\t- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"197d390e-c577-4a77-aead-08d0e3d6d0a4","uuid":"4c62e12e-b72a-4fb3-bdc1-deec2c587620","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:16:17.223Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — PRD-06 Bell Schedule & Timetable (SCH-006)\n\nTicket: SH-6 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-05T05:40:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and\n`package.json` confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict,\nPrisma 5.20 on PostgreSQL, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons, x-date-pickers, x-data-grid) with emotion,\nzod 3.23.8, date-fns 3.6.0 / date-fns-tz 3.2.0, Vitest 2.1.1 and Playwright 1.47.2.\nThe stack fits every FR/NFR. **This PRD adds exactly one runtime dependency, `@dnd-kit/core`.**\nThe ADRs below record only the choices the requirements force.\n\n**Workspace facts I checked** (they confirm intake or add detail to it):\n\n- `@dnd-kit/core` is not installed. The current npm release is `6.3.1`, with peer deps `react >=16.8` and `react-dom >=16.8`, so it is compatible with React 18.3.1.\n- No SWR, React Query or TanStack package is installed. Client data fetching in the codebase is plain `fetch` plus local state.\n- `prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => …)` is already used (sections-service, courses-service). No code uses `isolationLevel` or handles `P2034` yet, so this PRD sets the precedent.\n- `src/lib/dates.ts` already exports `formatInSchoolTz(date, pattern, timezone)`, which wraps `formatInTimeZone`.\n- `.eslintrc` `no-restricted-imports` bans `@fullcalendar/*`, `react-datepicker`, `notistack`, `sonner`, `@radix-ui/*` and others. `@dnd-kit/*` is not banned, so the lint rule needs no change.\n- Vitest runs `tests/unit/**` and `tests/integration/**`. Integration tests are gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (precedent: `tests/integration/email-dispatch.test.ts`, which also tests a concurrent-race scenario).\n- No `@media print` or `GlobalStyles` print rule exists yet.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the declared stack; add `@dnd-kit/core@6.3.1` (exact pin) and nothing else\n\n**Context.** FR-23 requires drag-and-drop to use `@dnd-kit/core` only. NFR-6 allows that one\nnon-visual dependency, plus `@dnd-kit/sortable` only if needed. FR-5 period reorder could\njustify sortable, but intake's default for FR-5 is up/down buttons.\n\n**Decision.** Add `\"@dnd-kit/core\": \"6.3.1\"` with no caret, matching the repo's exact-pin\nconvention (`\"@mui/material\": \"9.5.0\"`). Do **not** add `@dnd-kit/sortable`,\n`@dnd-kit/utilities` or `@dnd-kit/modifiers`. Period reorder (FR-5, FR-33 `POST /api/periods/reorder`)\nuses MUI `IconButton`s with ArrowUpward and ArrowDownward icons.\n\n**Consequences.** One new lockfile entry. Leaving out sortable keeps the reorder logic\nserver-side and simple. The grid uses only `DndContext`, `useDraggable`, `useDroppable`,\n`DragOverlay` and `PointerSensor`. The ESLint ban list stays as it is.\n\n## ADR-2: Store and compare times as zero-padded `'HH:MM'` strings; derive \"now\" with the existing date-fns-tz helper\n\n**Context.** FR-1 stores `startTime` and `endTime` as `'HH:MM'` strings in the school timezone.\nFR-6 needs overlap and end-after-start checks. FR-31 and FR-45 need the current period and\ntoday's ISO weekday in `SchoolSettings.timezone`. NFR-11 asks for tests that cover multiple timezones.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Zod (FR-39) enforces `/^([01]\\d|2[0-3]):[0-5]\\d$/`. Zero-padded 24-hour strings sort and compare correctly as plain strings, so `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the overlap test (FR-6). No parsing to `Date` is needed.\n- \"Now\" is `formatInSchoolTz(now, 'HH:mm', tz)` and the ISO weekday is `Number(formatInSchoolTz(now, 'i', tz))` (1=Mon…7=Sun, matching FR-2). The `period.startTime <= nowHHMM < period.endTime` comparison is again a string comparison (FR-31, FR-26 Now/Next, FR-24 highlight).\n- `getScheduleForDate` (FR-30) also derives the weekday with `'i'` in the school timezone.\n- MUI X `TimeField` (FR-5) binds a `Date` value. The form converts with date-fns `format(d, 'HH:mm')` and `parse(s, 'HH:mm', ref)`. Both functions are already installed, and the field's `Date` is wall-clock only.\n\n**Consequences.** No new date library and no timezone arithmetic on stored times. Unit tests\npass a fixed `now` and several `tz` values (e.g. `UTC`, `America/Los_Angeles`, `Asia/Kolkata`) into\npure functions. Ceiling: periods cannot cross midnight. That is acceptable for a school day,\nand the endTime-after-startTime check already rejects them.\n\n## ADR-3: Clash check and write in one SERIALIZABLE interactive transaction with bounded retry on `P2034`\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 requires clash validation and the insert or update to run in one SERIALIZABLE\ntransaction, with retry, so that two concurrent conflicting inserts yield exactly one success.\nNFR-12 needs an integration test for this. FR-37 requires that a clash saves nothing and returns 409.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Use `prisma.$transaction(fn, { isolationLevel: Prisma.TransactionIsolationLevel.Serializable })`, which Prisma 5.20 supports natively on PostgreSQL.\n- Retry the whole transaction up to 3 attempts when the error is `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` with `code === 'P2034'` (write conflict or deadlock). After the last attempt, rethrow so `withHandler` maps it to a 500. The retry loop is a small local function inside `src/server/timetable/timetable-service.ts`. Do not add a shared util until a second caller exists (NFR-9).\n- Clashes found inside the transaction throw `AppError('CONFLICT', msg, { clashes })` from `src/server/http.ts`. The throw rolls the transaction back, so nothing is saved (FR-37, NFR-8).\n- The FR-3 `@@unique([courseId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` stays as a last line of defence. Map `P2002` to 409 as well.\n- Teaching-day switch-off (FR-7) and period reorder (FR-5) also use one transaction, at the default isolation level. Reorder needs a two-phase write because `Period.order @unique` would collide mid-update: first set each order to `-(i+1)`, then to `i+1`.\n\n**Consequences.** Clash rules (FR-9–FR-13) must run their reads through `tx`, not the global\n`prisma`, or SERIALIZABLE protects nothing. `validateSlot` therefore takes a client parameter\nthat defaults to `prisma`, so FR-36 availability can reuse the same logic outside a write.\nThe NFR-12 test follows the `email-dispatch.test.ts` pattern: `describe.runIf(TEST_DATABASE_URL)`,\nthen `Promise.allSettled` of two conflicting creates, asserting exactly one is fulfilled.\n\n## ADR-4: Clash detection loads candidate slots with set-based Prisma queries and checks them in memory (no raw SQL)\n\n**Context.** FR-9–FR-14 define four clash rules plus the non-class period and non-teaching day\nrule, and `validateSlot` must return all of them. FR-36 availability must cover every\n(day, period) cell for one course in under 300 ms (NFR-3). The section week must load in under\n400 ms (NFR-2). The FR-3 index `@@index([academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId])` already exists for this.\n\n**Decision.**\n- **validateSlot** (single cell): one `findMany` of the other slots at `(academicYearId, dayOfWeek, periodId)`, excluding the moving slot's id on PATCH (FR-14). The query includes `course { teacherId, coTeacherIds, sectionId, code, subject, section }`, `room` and `period`. The teacher, room and section rules (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11) are array checks in TypeScript. Student overlap (FR-12) is one `enrollment.groupBy` / `count` over `status: 'ACTIVE'`, with `courseId in [otherCourseIds]` and `studentId in` the new course's active student ids. Both are covered by the existing `@@index([studentId, status])`.\n- **Availability** (FR-36): load all slots in the course's academic year in one query, and the new course's ACTIVE student ids plus the ACTIVE enrollments of those students in one query. Then run the same pure rule functions for each of the up-to-84 cells (7 × 12) in memory. This is a fixed number of queries, not one per cell.\n- The rule functions are pure (`(candidate, others, overlapCounts) => string[]`), so NFR-11 unit tests need no database.\n- **Editor and personal grids** (FR-35, FR-38): one `findMany` per view with `include` (course → subject, teacher; room; period). There are no N+1 queries.\n\n**Consequences.** No `$queryRaw`, so everything stays type-safe under strict TS (NFR-10).\nCeiling: availability does its work in memory over the year's slots, roughly a few thousand rows\nfor a single school (NFR-16), which fits the target comfortably.\n`// ponytail: in-memory clash map over the year's slots; move to SQL aggregation if slot count reaches ~50k.`\n\n## ADR-5: The drag-and-drop grid uses `@dnd-kit/core` with pessimistic updates; keyboard users get an MUI Dialog path\n\n**Context.** FR-16–FR-23 describe the editor grid. FR-20 requires that a rejected drop snaps\nback. FR-19 tints clashing cells while dragging, and NFR-4 says colour must never be the only signal.\nFR-22 and NFR-4 require a keyboard alternative. NFR-5 and NFR-6 allow MUI-only visuals.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `DndContext` with `PointerSensor` only (activation distance about 5px, so a click still opens the FR-21 room Popover). Cells use `useDroppable`. BREAK/LUNCH rows and non-teaching days set `disabled: true` (FR-16). Course cards and placed slots use `useDraggable`. The drag preview is a `DragOverlay` holding an MUI `Paper` (FR-23).\n- **Pessimistic updates.** The grid changes only after the POST or PATCH succeeds. On a 409, nothing moves, so \"snap back\" (FR-20) needs no rollback code. The toast comes from `useToast().showToast({ message, severity: 'error' })` with `details.clashes` joined into the message.\n- The clash map from `GET /api/timetable/availability` is fetched once when a drag starts, cached per courseId while the editor is open, and dropped after any mutation. Cells in the map get a red tint, an ErrorOutline icon and an MUI `Tooltip` listing the messages (FR-19, NFR-4).\n- Keyboard: cells are `tabIndex=0`. Enter on an empty cell opens an MUI `Dialog` listing the section's courses (FR-22). Delete on a focused slot opens the existing `ConfirmDialog` (FR-18). dnd-kit's `KeyboardSensor` is **not** used, because the Dialog path is the required alternative and it avoids the cost of making sensor announcements accessible.\n- Data fetching uses plain `fetch` plus `useState`, as elsewhere in the repo. No SWR or React Query is added.\n\n**Consequences.** Each drop costs one round-trip of latency (no optimistic UI). That is acceptable\nfor an admin tool, and it removes a whole class of state-desync bugs. Every component must still\nbe looked up through mui-mcp before use (NFR-5). The mui-mcp server is connected in this\nenvironment, so the intake blocker is cleared for now, but dev must still call it.\n\n## ADR-6: Print layout uses native `@media print` and `window.print()`, with no PDF or print library\n\n**Context.** FR-29 requires a print-friendly A4 landscape page containing only the grid, the entity\nname and the academic year. NFR-16 requires print to work in the last two versions of Chrome, Edge,\nFirefox and Safari. NFR-6 bans extra UI libraries.\n\n**Decision.** The Print button calls `window.print()`. An MUI `GlobalStyles` block, rendered with\nthe grid, contains `@page { size: A4 landscape; margin: 10mm }`. Under `@media print`, an `sx` rule\nhides the app shell (nav drawer, app bar, sidebar, view selectors and buttons), and a print-only\nheading shows the entity name and academic year. Cells use `break-inside: avoid`. Cell background\ncolours need `print-color-adjust: exact`. Course code and teacher text carry the meaning anyway,\nso colour is not required (NFR-4).\n\n**Consequences.** No dependency is added. Firefox and Safari ignore some `@page` size hints,\nwhich is acceptable because the user picks the orientation in the print dialog. The Playwright\ncheck uses `page.emulateMedia({ media: 'print' })` and asserts that the shell is hidden.\n\n## ADR-7: Testing stays on the existing Vitest and Playwright setup\n\n**Context.** NFR-11 (unit tests), NFR-12 (concurrency integration test), NFR-13 (e2e test) and NFR-10 (quality gate).\n\n**Decision.**\n- `tests/unit/timetable-clash.test.ts`: the pure rule functions from ADR-4, period overlap validation (FR-6) and `getCurrentSlot`/Now-Next with injected `now` and `tz` (ADR-2).\n- `tests/integration/timetable-concurrency.test.ts`: `// @vitest-environment node`, gated on `TEST_DATABASE_URL` (ADR-3).\n- `tests/e2e/timetable.spec.ts`: Playwright tests for dragging a course into a cell, a clash toast on a conflicting drop, and the student Today view, all against the FR-41–FR-44 seed. dnd-kit's PointerSensor works with Playwright `mouse.down/move/up` when there are intermediate `move` steps. `locator.dragTo` alone may not cross the activation distance.\n\n**Consequences.** No new test tooling. The e2e tests depend on the seed fixtures (FR-44) being idempotent.\n\n---\n\n## Downstream notes (no stack impact; for `arch-design-system` to decide)\n\n- FR-21 free rooms: no stack choice is needed. Either intake's suggested `GET /api/timetable/free-rooms` or the availability payload works with ADR-4's query pattern. One query (rooms not archived, minus `roomId`s used at that day and period) is enough.\n- FR-7 slot count: intake's default (`GET /api/school-days` returns `slotCount` per day) is one `timetableSlot.groupBy({ by: ['dayOfWeek'] })`, which works fine with this stack.\n- FR-40 audit: `audit()` from `src/server/audit.ts` should be called inside the same transaction client where its signature allows. That is for design to confirm.\n","numLines":146,"startLine":1,"totalLines":146}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01YBpLLfQTfXf3Czc38c5xPm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
